hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"15126","Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar said: I do not care that he gives me from it, meaning: If you contract him while you do not know that he will give you except from begging people."
"15127","Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: The sheep given in charity to Barirah, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ate from it."
"15128","From al-Thawri and Israel ibn Yunus, or one of them, from Abu Jafar al-Farra, he said: Jafar ibn Abi Tharwan al-Harithi narrated to me from Abu al-Tayyah that he came to Ali and said to him: I want to contract manumission. He said: Have you anything? He said: No. So Ali gathered them and said: Help your brother. They collected for him, and a remainder of his contract remained for him. He brought it to Ali and asked him about the surplus, and Ali said: Put it among the contracted slaves."
"15129","From Mamar, he said: A man informed me, he said: A contracted slave used to sit with al-Hasan and asked him to seek help for him, so al-Hasan spoke to his companions and said: Help your brother. They helped him, he paid his contract, and a surplus remained for him. He asked al-Hasan about it, and he said: Do you need it? He said: Yes, so he ordered him to spend it."
"15130","From Israel ibn Yunus from Abu Jafar al-Farra from Abu Layla al-Kindi, he said: A slave of his came to Salman and said: Contract me. He said: Have you anything? He said: No, except I beg people. He said: Do you want to feed me the washings of peoples hands? And he disliked to contract him."
"15131","From Abd Allah ibn al-Muharrar, he said: Nafi informed me that a contracted slave of Ibn Umar brought him an installment that had fallen due upon him, and he said to him: From where did you bring this? He said: I begged people. He said: You brought me the filth of people to feed me? Ibn Umar returned it to him and freed him."
"15132","From al-Thawri from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari from Nafi from Ibn Umar: That he used to dislike contracting his slave when he had no craft, saying: Will you feed me from the filth of people?"
"15133","From Mamar, he said: A man from the people of Syria informed me that they found in the treasury of Hims a letter from Umar ibn al-Khattab to Umayr ibn Sad al-Ansari, and he was his governor, and in it: To proceed, those before you ransom their slaves through the begging of people."
"15134","Mamar reported: Qatadah used to dislike, when the slave had no craft nor any means in anything, that a man contract him; he contracts him only to beg people."
"15135","Mamar reported from Qatadah, he said: A man from the people of Basrah used to buy the slave-girl for ten dinars or about that, then contract her and leave her, and she would beg people, and Qatadah used to forbid that."
"15136","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ata ibn al-Saib informed me that Abd Allah ibn Habib informed him from Ali ibn Abi Talib from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: And give them from the wealth of Allah which He gave you, he said: A quarter of the contract. Ibn Jurayj said: And more than one informed me from Ata ibn al-Saib that he used to narrate this hadith without mentioning the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in it."
"15137","Mamar reported from Ata ibn al-Saib from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami that Ali said about His saying: And give them from the wealth of Allah which He gave you, he said: A quarter of his contract is left for the contracted slave."
"15138","From al-Thawri from Abd al-Ala, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami narrated to us, and I witnessed him contract a slave of his for four thousand, and he remitted a thousand from him in the last of his installments, then he said: And I heard Ali saying: And give them from the wealth of Allah which He gave you, he said: A quarter of what you contract them upon."
"15139","From al-Thawri from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bashir, he said: Fadalah ibn Abi Umayyah narrated to me from his father, and Umar ibn al-Khattab had contracted him, he said: So I borrowed two hundred from Hafsah for his stipend, and she aided me with them. He said: So I mentioned to her, he said: I said: Are you only aiding me with them, will you not make them due upon me? She said: I fear I will not live to that. Abd al-Malik said: So I mentioned that to Ikrimah, and he said: That is the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: And give them from the wealth of Allah which He gave you."
"15140","From al-Thawri from Mughirah from Ibrahim, he said: This is something people were urged to, about His saying: And give them from the wealth of Allah which He gave you, regarding the master and others."
"15141","Mamar reported from Ibn Abi Najih from Mujahid, he said: A portion of his contract is left for him. Mamar said: And Qatadah used to say: It is a tenth left for him from his contract."
"15142","Al-Thawri reported from Salim al-Aftas from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: Ibn Umar, when he contracted a slave, used to dislike remitting for him in the first of his installments except in the last of it, fearing he would be unable."
"15143","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: It is said: Muslims are upon their conditions in what accords with the truth. He said: And Ata was asked about a man contracted with his family stipulating that we have a share in your inheritance, he said: No, the condition of Allah is before their condition."
"15144","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man from the people of Iraq mentioned to me that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote about this to Adi: Do not allow the condition of his family; the right of Allah is more deserving."
"15145","From al-Thawri, he said: If they stipulate upon the contracted slave that we have a share in your inheritance, their condition is void, nothing."
"15146","Mamar reported from Ayyub from Muhammad ibn Sirin, he said: A dispute was brought to Shurayh, and the one who contracted said: I contracted this slave of mine stipulating his clientage, his house, his inheritance and his offspring. He said: So Shurayh voided that, and the man said: What use is my condition for thirty years? Shurayh said: The condition of Allah is more deserving before your condition; He stipulated it upon the tongue of His Prophet Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, fifty years ago."
"15147","From Subayh, he said: I asked Said ibn Jubayr, and it had been stipulated upon me, while I was contracted, that I not go out, and Said said: They made the land shares upon you; go out."
"15148","From al-Thawri from Ismail from al-Shabi, he said: If it is stipulated upon the contracted slave that he not go out, he goes out if he wishes, and if it is stipulated upon him that he not marry, he does not marry except with his masters permission."
"15149","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If they stipulate upon him that your house is our house, he said: Not allowed. I said: And they stipulate that you serve us after you are freed for a month, he said: Allowed. And Amr ibn Dinar said: I see everything they stipulate in his contract as allowed upon him when he is freed."
"15150","From al-Thawri from Jabir from al-Shabi, he said: If they stipulate upon him that he not go out, he goes out if he wishes. And Sufyan said: He does not marry except with his masters permission."
"15151","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is everything stipulated upon the contracted slave for his family after he is freed void? He said: Yes."
"15152","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A contracted woman whose family stipulates upon her that whatever child you bear in your contract, they are slaves, he said: Allowed if she stipulated with what she bore in it. And about a man contracting with his master stipulating upon him that whatever child you beget, they are slaves to me, he said: They belong to his master."
"15153","From al-Thawri, he said: If they stipulate that whatever child you beget of slaves, they are slaves."
"15154","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I say: That condition is allowed; do you not see that the contracted slave stipulating that my clientage is to whom I wish is allowed."
"15155","Mamar reported from Qatadah, he said: When the master stipulates upon his contracted slave a gift of a ram every year, it is allowed."
"15156","From Mamar, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: Muslims are upon their conditions in what accords with the truth."
"15157","Mamar reported from Ayyub, he said: Iyas ibn Muawiyah informed me that Adi ibn Artah asked him and al-Hasan about a man who contracted his slave stipulating that I have a share in your wealth when you die, he said: So I said: It is allowed. And al-Hasan said: It is nothing. He said: So Adi wrote about it to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and he wrote with the like of al-Hasans saying: It is nothing. He said: Iyas had me read the letter when it came to him."
"15158","Hisham ibn Hassan reported from Muhammad that a woman came to Shurayh and said: I freed my slave upon his rendering to me ten dirhams every month as long as I live. Shurayh said: Your manumission is valid and your condition is void. And Ibn Jurayj mentioned it from Ibn Sirin."
"15159","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ayyub ibn Musa, he said: Nafi informed me from Abd Allah ibn Umar that Umar ibn al-Khattab freed every praying one of the Arab captives, and made it decisive over them, and stipulated upon them that you serve the caliph after me three years, and stipulated upon them that he keeps you with the like of what I kept you with. He said: So al-Khiyar bought that three-year service of his from Uthman for Abu Farwah, and Uthman freed al-Khiyar, and he left, and Uthman took Abu Farwah."
"15160","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me from Nafi: That it was in the will of Umar ibn al-Khattab that every Arab be freed from the wealth of Allah, and for the commander after him three years over them, managing them about as Umar managed them. Nafi said: Abd Allah used to say: Rather he freed every Muslim of the treasurys slaves."
"15161","And I heard Abu Hanifah, and he was asked about a man who said to his slave: When you render to me a hundred dinars you are free, he said: When he renders, he is free, and his master takes the rest of his wealth."
"15162","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me from Nafi that Ibn Umar freed a slave of his stipulating upon him that you serve me two years, and he pastured for him part of a year, then came to him with his horses, either in a pilgrimage or an umrah, and Abd Allah said to him: I have left for you what I stipulated upon you, and you are free, no work upon you."
"15163","Mamar reported from Ayyub from Amr ibn Dinar, and Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed me from Amr ibn Dinar: That Ali gave in charity part of his land, making it charity after his death, and freed slaves of his, stipulating upon them that you work in this wealth five years."
"15164","From Ibn Jurayj from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Those who passed used to stipulate upon their contracted slaves that we have your release-dues on the day you are freed. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: Every condition at the manumission contract is allowed."
"15165","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab said: His condition stands for him until he pays his contract, and when he pays his contract, no condition upon him."
"15166","Mamar reported from al-Zuhri, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab freed every Muslim of the treasurys slaves, stipulating upon them that you serve the caliph after me three years, and that he keeps you with the like of what I kept you with. So al-Khiyar bought his service from him, meaning from Uthman, the three years, for his slave Abu Farwah."
"15167","Mamar reported from Qatadah that Ibn al-Musayyib said: When he says: You are free, you are the freed one, every condition after it is void."
"15168","Mamar reported from Ibn Shubrumah, he said: When a man says to his slave: You are free upon serving me ten years, his condition stands for him. From Mamar from al-Zuhri, he said: One who heard al-Hasan and Ikrimah and al-Hakam ibn Utaybah informed me, and a man informed me from Ibn al-Musayyib, they said the like of it."
"15169","From a man of Qays, he said: I asked Abu Hanifah: Is it written in the contract of the contracted slave that you not go out except with my permission? He said: No. I said: Why? He said: Because he may not prevent him from the bounty of Allah and going out to seek. I said: Is it written that you not marry except with my permission? He said: If he writes it, good, and if he does not write it, he may not marry except with his permission. I said: Is it binding in your view even if he does not stipulate that upon him? He said: Yes. I said: Do you accept it when it comes to other than you? He said: Yes."
"15170","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man contracted his slave, or settled with him, concealing wealth, slaves, cash or other than that, he said: It belongs to the slave. Amr ibn Dinar and Sulayman ibn Musa said it."
"15171","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: And if his master asked him about his wealth and he concealed it, he said: It belongs to his master. And Amr ibn Dinar and Sulayman ibn Musa said it. I said to Ata: Why do you two differ? He said: It is because there is not in his child the like of his wealth."
"15172","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if his master knew of the slaves child and the master did not mention him, nor the slave, at the contracting, he said: He is not in his contract; he is the wealth of their master. And Amr ibn Dinar said it. And the mother of the child is in his contract, he said: They are slaves. And Ibn Jurayj said it from Ata and Amr ibn Dinar and Sulayman ibn Musa."
"15173","Mamar reported from Qatadah from al-Hasan about a man contracting a slave of his, and he has a child by his bondwoman, and the master did not know, and the mother of the child is in his contract, he said: He contracted only upon his household and his wealth, so his child is of his wealth."
"15174","From al-Thawri from Mansur from Ibrahim about a man who contracted his slave, and he has a concubine and a child, and his master did not know of them, Ibrahim said: His concubine remains as she was, and his child are slaves of the master who contracted him. He said: And Sufyan used to hold to it: When a man contracts his slave or sells him, the wealth belongs to the master."
"15175","Mamar reported from Ayyub from Ibn Sirin from Shurayh, he said: The child of the contracted woman is at the station of their mother; if she is freed they are freed, and if she is enslaved they are enslaved. Mamar reported from Mughirah from Ibrahim the like of Shurayhs saying."
"15176","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The contracted slave does not stipulate that whatever child I beget is of my contract; he and his master are silent, mentioning nothing of what is born to him of a child, then a child is born to him, he said: He is in his contract. And Amr ibn Dinar said it."
"15177","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me that a woman was contracted, then bore two sons after she was contracted, then died, and Ibn al-Zubayr was asked about her, and he said: If they uphold their mothers contract they are freed. And Amr ibn Dinar said it, and did not attribute it to Ibn al-Zubayr."
"15178","From al-Thawri about a contracted slave who died leaving wealth and a child by a contracted woman, with a remainder of his contract upon him, he said: His child strives for what remains of his contract, and they are freed with his freeing, and if they are unable they become slaves. And Hammad used to say: Their minority is inability, no waiting with them, meaning: They are owned when their father dies. Sufyan said: If they do not render the terms, their minority is inability."
"15179","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ata was asked about the contracted slave who takes a concubine secretly, and a child is born to him, and he dies leaving them minors, leaving wealth, he said: The majority of his child is not awaited with the wealth."
"15180","Mamar and al-Thawri reported from Ayyub from Ibn Sirin from Shurayh: That he was asked about the child of the contracted woman, and he said: Her child is like her; if she is freed they are freed, and if she is enslaved they are enslaved. Mamar reported from Mughirah from Ibrahim the like of Shurayhs saying."
"15181","Mamar and al-Thawri reported from Mughirah from Ibrahim, he said: No harm in the contracted slave being sold for manumission, and he saw no harm in the child of the contracted woman being sold for manumission, she seeking help with him in her contract."
"15182","From al-Thawri, he said: If the contracted slave had a son buy him from other than his master who contracted him, then he is unable, he said: His son is enslaved and does not strive, he said: Because he did not enter into his contract."
"15183","From al-Thawri about a man who had a pregnant bondwoman, and he freed her before his death, then died, and she remained days then died and her child remained, he said: No striving upon the child, because he was freed only with his mothers freeing."
"15184","From al-Thawri, he said: When the contracted woman is freed her child is freed, when they are born in her contract, and when the mother of the child is freed her child is not freed. Mamar said: It reached me that when the master knows of the contracted slaves child and the master does not mention them in the contract, they are slaves."
"15185","Mamar reported from al-Zuhri about the contracted slave who takes a concubine secretly, and a child is born to him, then dies leaving them minors, he said: If they uphold their fathers contract, otherwise they are slaves. And Qatadah said it. Al-Zuhri said: When the contracted slave dies leaving minor children, he said: If they uphold their fathers contract, otherwise they are slaves."
"15186","From Ibn Jurayj from Ata, he said: When a slave of yours contracts, and he has sons that day, and he contracts with you for himself and for them, and their father dies or one of them dies, his value on the day he dies is laid down from the contract, and if he frees him or some of his sons, likewise. He said: And Amr ibn Dinar said to me the like of that. I said to Amr: What if what died or was freed is worth the whole contract or more? He said: He and his sons are valued, as if they reached six hundred dinars, and their contract was upon two hundred dinars, so I drop the price of what was freed or died: a sixth of the value, a hundred dinars, and two hundred dinars a third of it, so a third or a sixth of it is laid down from the two hundred of their contract."
"15187","Ibn Jurayj said: As for Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah, he said: If a man contracts a man and sons of his that day all together, not singling a contract upon any of them, they are equal in it, the excellent and the not excellent, the woman and the man, and whoever of them dies, their portions are equal."
"15188","Mamar reported from Qatadah about a man contracting for himself and his sons, then the father dies, or one of them dies or is freed, he said: If it is written in their contract: their living for their dead, it is upon the remainder, nothing is dropped from them for the dead, and if their contract was unrestricted, the value of the dead is dropped from them. And the people of Kufah say: It is nothing, the wealth of so-and-so bore for the wealth of so-and-so. Mamar mentioned it from Hammad and Ibn Shubrumah."
"15189","From Mamar, he said: It reached me about a contracted slave contracted upon himself and his sons, then their father dies, or one of them dies, that if their father dies or one of them dies, his value is appraised on the day he contracted him according to the contract, and laid down from them according to the contract, and if freed, likewise, and if the bondwoman is freed her child is freed when they occur after his contract."
"15190","From al-Thawri from Mansur from Ibrahim, he said: When a household contracts one contract, whoever of them dies, the wealth is upon the remainder of them."
"15191","From al-Thawri about a man who contracted slaves of his for a thousand dirhams, it is upon them all; whoever of them dies, the other strives with it, except that each person singles what is upon him, and if one of them is freed he is appraised by his value, then dropped from them all on the day they were contracted. And his saying: If he says in his condition: their living for their dead, alike."
"15192","From Mamar, he said: I know no one differing about a man contracting, he and his wife, or he and his sons all together, and one of them is freed, that he is freed according to the contract, and if he has sons who did not enter the contract on the day he was contracted, born after the contract, and one of them is freed, nothing is dropped from them for him. And Mamar said: And it reached me that al-Hasan used to say it."
"15193","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: And if you contract him with no child for him, then a child is born to him by a concubine of his, and their father dies, nothing is laid down from them, and if one of them is freed, nothing is freed from them for him, because he was not in his fathers contract."
"15194","From Ibn Jurayj from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I said to him: I contracted him the day I contracted him with no child for him, then a child occurred for him, and they were in his contract, and their father died, he said: They are upon their fathers contract, nothing is laid down from them for him. I said: And one of his sons died, he said: Nothing is laid down from their father. I said: And I freed their father, he said: His sons are freed. I said: And I freed one of his sons, he said: Nothing is laid down from their father."
"15195","Mamar reported from Qatadah, he said: If a child is born to the contracted slave after his contract, and that child of his is freed or dies, nothing is dropped from him for him."
"15196","From al-Thawri, he said: When the contracted woman is freed her child is freed, when they are born in her contract, and when the mother of the child is freed her child is not freed until her master dies."
"15197","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me about the contracted slave whose child dies: His master takes his wealth. He said: And Ata said to me about a contracted slave whose daughter died, he used to pay for her: Her inheritance is for her father, because he used to pay for her."
"15198","From Mamar about the contracted slave whose daughter dies, he used to render for her, he said: Her inheritance is for her father, from more than one."
"15199","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The contracted slave dies leaving free children and leaving more than what remains upon him of his contract, he said: What remains of his contract is paid for him, and what is surplus is for his sons. I said: Did this reach you from anyone? He said: They claimed Ali used to judge with that. He said: As for Ibn Umar, he used to say: It all belongs to his master, whatever he left."
"15200","From Ibn Uyaynah and Ibn al-Taymi from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid from Amir al-Shabi, he said: Ibn Masud used to say about the contracted slave: When he dies leaving wealth, the remainder of his contract is rendered for him, and what is surplus is returned to his child, if he has free children. Amir said: And Shurayh used to judge with that also."
"15201","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ibn Tawus from his father, he said: The remainder of his contract is paid, then what remains is for his free children. Mamar said: And one who heard al-Hasan informed me, he says the like of that. Mamar reported from al-Zuhri that Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan judged with the like of that, and I know it only from Raja ibn Haywah."
"15202","From Ibrahim ibn Umar from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah from Ibrahim and Amir and al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin, they said: The remainder of his contract is paid, and what remains is for his free children."
"15203","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah mentioning that Abbad, the freedman of al-Mutawakkil, died contracted, having paid half of his contract, leaving much wealth, and a free daughter of his whose mother was free, so Abd al-Malik wrote: That what remains of his contract be paid, and what remains of his wealth between his daughter and his masters. And Amr said to me: I see it only for his daughter."
"15204","From al-Thawri from Mansur, he said: I asked Ibrahim about a man who contracted his slave, and the contracted slave died without rendering anything, leaving wealth, he said: The masters are given their contract, and what remains of wealth is paid to his heirs."
"15205","Mamar reported from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: When the contracted slave dies leaving free children, the wealth belongs to his master. From Ibn Jurayj from al-Zuhri the like of it, he said: And his free children and free wife have nothing."
"15206","Mamar reported from Simak, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz was written to about a contracted slave dying leaving free children, and he wrote: He contracted only with his masters wealth, so he and his wealth belong to his master until he is freed."
"15207","Mamar reported from Qatadah from Mabad al-Juhani, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan asked me about the contracted slave dying leaving free children, with wealth more than what remains upon him, and I said to him: Umar ibn al-Khattab and Muawiyah judged in it with two judgments, and Muawiyahs judgment in it is dearer to me than Umars judgment. He said: Why? I said: Because Dawud was better than Sulayman, yet Sulayman understood it. Umar judged that all his wealth belongs to his master, and Muawiyah judged that his master is given the remainder of his contract, then what remains is for his free children. Mamar reported from Ismail Abu al-Miqdam that he heard Ikrimah narrating that Muawiyah judged with it."
"15208","Al-Thawri reported from Tariq from al-Shabi from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: All the wealth belongs to the master."
"15209","From Mamar from al-Zuhri, he said: When he has children with him in his contract, and children not in his contract, he renders what remains of his contract, then divides between them what remains of his wealth upon their shares."
"15210","Al-Thawri reported: Simak informed me from Qabus ibn Mukhariq from his father, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr wrote to Ali asking him about two Muslims who became heretics, and about a Muslim who committed adultery with a Christian woman, and about a contracted slave who died leaving a remainder of his contract and leaving free children, and he wrote to him: As for the two who became heretics, if they repent, otherwise strike their necks, and as for the Muslim who committed adultery with a Christian woman, establish the penalty upon him and hand the Christian woman to her coreligionists, and as for the contracted slave, give his masters the remainder of his contract and give his free children what remains of his wealth. From Israel from Simak ibn Harb from Qabus ibn Mukhariq from his father that Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr wrote to Ali, then he mentioned the like of it about the contracted slave."
"15211","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I asked Ata about a slave between two men, one of them freed half of him, and the other withheld, then he died, he said: His inheritance is two halves between them. And Amr ibn Dinar said it. Mamar said from Ayyub: Ayyub ibn Muawiyah judged with the like of Atas saying."
"15212","Mamar reported from Qatadah, he said: His inheritance belongs to the one who freed, and he guarantees his companion his price."
"15213","Mamar said, and al-Zuhri said: His inheritance belongs to the one who withheld."
"15214","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A slave, a third of him free and a third of him in a contract, and he died leaving more than his contract, and he is as if singling the one who collected his contract, then he said to me after: His inheritance belongs to the one who withheld, and the one who freed has nothing of them, when he renders the remainder of his contract. And Amr ibn Dinar said: Thirds."
"15215","Mamar reported from Qatadah, he said: Sulayman ibn Hisham asked me, and al-Zuhri, about a slave, one of them freed, one of them contracted, and one of them withheld, and al-Zuhri said: The one who freed has nothing of his inheritance; it belongs to the one who withheld, and for the one who contracted between them two halves. Qatadah said: And I said: If the contract was after the manumission it is nothing, and if it was before the manumission, the one who withheld has a third of his price upon the one who freed, and two thirds of the clientage are for the manumitter, and a third for the one who contracted. And the saying of al-Thawri: The one who freed guarantees when he did not guarantee on the day of the contract."
"15216","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab said: Slavery overcomes lineage, so it is for manumission more overcome."
"15217","From Ibn Jurayj from Mamar from Ibn Tawus from his father, he said: His inheritance and his clientage are thirds."
"15218","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, he said about the contracted slave freed in part and not freed in part, then he dies, he said: No, his divorce, his wounds and his testimony are at the station of a slave."
"15219","Mamar reported from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: The contracted slave, his testimony, his wounds, his divorce and his blood-money are at the station of the slave."
"15220","From al-Thawri about two men with a slave between them, and one of them permitted the other to contract his share, then the other freed, he said: The manumission is deferred until it is seen what the slave does; if he is unable, the manumitter guarantees, and if he renders the contract, the one who contracted guarantees for the one who freed."
"15221","From Mamar about a slave between two men, one of them freed his share, then the other freed after, he said: As for al-Zuhri and Amr ibn Dinar, they said: His clientage and his inheritance are between them in halves. As for Ibn Shubrumah, he said: His clientage and his inheritance belong to the first, because he had guaranteed him when he freed him."
"15222","From Ibn Uyaynah from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Said informed me, he said: There was a slave of the household of Abu al-As, they inherited him, and they freed him except a man among them, so he interceded with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he gave him to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, freed him. So he used to say: I am the freedman of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"15223","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I said to Ata: The contracted slave, if he commits an offense, who is taken with it? He said: His master. Amr ibn Dinar said it, and Ata said to me: It is for his master upon him."
"15224","Mamar reported from al-Zuhri, he said: When the contracted slave kills a man by mistake, his contract and his clientage go to the slain one, except that his master ransom him."
"15225","From al-Thawri, our companions said: The offense of the contracted slave is upon himself, as when he is afflicted with something it is his, and if he is wounded with a wound it is upon him in his value, not exceeding his value. Abd al-Razzaq said: And with it we take."
"15226","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, from al-Hasan, who said: His offense is borne upon his own neck."
"15227","Al-Thawri reported from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, who said: The offense of the mukatab, the mudabbar, and the umm al-walad lies upon the master until he releases them, just as he bound them."
"15228","Ma'mar reported from one of his companions, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, who said: The offense of the mukatab is upon his master; if his master wishes, he surrenders him. He said: And this is the most beloved of their sayings to me."
"15229","From al-Hasan ibn Umara, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, who said: His master guarantees his value."
"15230","Al-Hakam said, and al-Sha'bi said: His master guarantees all of it. And al-Hakam said: His offense is a debt which he works to pay off."
"15231","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: If the mukatab is struck by some injury, to whom does its retaliation belong? He said: To the mukatab; so used to say those before you. I said: What if the mukatab's master wants to surrender the mukatab for what he committed? He said: That is his, if he wishes. And Ma'mar said the like of that, though he did not mention it from Ata."
"15232","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: If the mukatab brings upon his master an offense involving one hundred dinars while he is worth two hundred dinars, or brings an offense involving one hundred dinars while he is worth fifty, does he not surrender him in each case if he wishes? He said: Yes."
"15233","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, from al-Hasan, who said: The offense of the mukatab is borne in his own neck."
"15234","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: I said to him: The mukatab is struck by some injury. He said: It belongs to the mukatab. And Amr ibn Dinar said it. I said to Ata: Is it because he safeguards it from his wealth just as he safeguarded his wealth? He said: Yes."
"15235","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: The offense of the umm al-walad and the mudabbar is upon their master. From Ma'mar, from one of our companions, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, the like of it. From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, like his first hadith. Al-Thawri said: As for us, we say: It is in his neck, meaning the mukatab."
"15236","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri and Qatada, who both said: The blood-money of the umm al-walad is the blood-money of her mother, and her master pays blood-money on her behalf."
"15237","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Shubruma, who said: Whoever contracts kitaba for a share of a slave, or settles with him, he pays nothing to this one except that he pays the like of it to these others, unless he frees; the one who contracted him or freed him guarantees him."
"15238","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: I settled with my mukatab for some wealth upon him, and neither I nor he mentioned manumission. He said: I do not see him but as a debtor for what you settled with him upon. I said: Then he failed. He said: I do not see him but as a debtor who has been freed. And Amr ibn Dinar said something like that. Then I asked Ata afterwards, and he said: He is a slave until he pays the last of what is upon him. I said: Then he failed to pay it. He said: He is a slave until he pays the last of what is upon him; nothing frees him before he pays."
"15239","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who contracted his slave for one thousand dirhams, then settled with him for five hundred: he said: If he fails to pay the five hundred, he becomes a slave; and if he testifies while he is striving to pay, his testimony is valid."
"15240","From al-Thawri, who said: If a slave is owned between two men, and one of them contracts him without the permission of his partner, then when he pays what he contracted upon, this one becomes a partner with him in what he took from him, and the slave is freed, and the one who contracted guarantees the share of the other. If the one who contracted has means, it is taken from him; and if he has no means, the slave works to pay half his value, and he becomes his partner in what he took from his kitaba."
"15241","Ma'mar reported from Jabir al-Ju'fi, from Amir al-Sha'bi, who said: Whoever contracts a share of his in a slave with the permission of his partners, then he is freed, the slave works to pay what remains for his partners, and the one who contracted him does not guarantee him. Ma'mar said: And Ibn Shubruma said: If he settles or contracts, he guarantees. Ma'mar said: And it is more beloved to me."
"15242","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, concerning a mukatab owned among partners, with whom one of them settled: he said: The one who settled with him does not guarantee them, and he pays to the others what remains for them. Qatada said: Every kitaba that was before the manumission, there is no guarantee in it upon the one who settled."
"15243","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: A mukatab owned among a people, and he wants to settle with some of them? He said: No, unless he has of wealth the like of what he settled upon with these."
"15244","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a slave between two men, both of whom contracted him, and he paid to one of them his kitaba while he strives for the other in his kitaba: he said: His hadd punishment, his divorce, his inheritance, and his testimony are at the station of the slave, until he pays to the other. If he dies before paying him, then his inheritance belongs to him."
"15245","Ma'mar reported from Qatada and Ibn Shubruma, who both said: When he is striving to pay, he is at the station of the freeman, and his inheritance afterwards is for the one to whom he owes, and his wala is shared among them by portions."
"15246","From Ma'mar, he was asked about three men who settled with a mukatab of theirs, stipulating upon him that if you do not pay such-and-such, then you are a slave. He said: If he fails to pay anything of what they named upon him, he returns a slave."
"15247","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: A mukatab owned a slave and contracted him, then the slave's slave was freed, then he died; whose is his inheritance? He said: Those before you used to say: It belongs to the one who contracted him, he helps himself with it in his kitaba."
"15248","From al-Thawri, concerning a mukatab who contracted for one thousand dirhams, then the mukatab contracted a slave of his for two thousand, and the payer of the thousand paid five hundred, and the payer of the two thousand paid one thousand, then the first died: he said: What remains upon the survivor passes to the master, and the heirs of the first get nothing."
"15249","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who contracted a slave of his for two thousand, and the slave contracted a slave of his for two thousand, then the mukatab's mukatab died leaving four thousand: he said: The mukatab takes the two thousand upon which he contracted, and what remains belongs to the master."
"15250","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who contracted a slave of his for four thousand, then the mukatab bought a slave, then the slave bought himself from the mukatab and was freed: he said: The wala belongs to the master, the master of the mukatab. Al-Thawri said: And whatever the mukatab gifted, or gave in charity, or freed, then failed to pay, it is revoked."
"15251","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, that he was asked about the mukatab freeing a slave. He said: Should he not begin with himself?"
"15252","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, concerning a slave owned by a people who permitted him to buy a slave, then he freed him, then they sold him: they said: The wala belongs to the first ones who permitted."
"15253","Isra'il ibn Yunus reported, saying: Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay' told me, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, who said: A man contracted a youth for uqiyas he named, scheduling them upon him as installments. The slave came to him with all his wealth, but he refused to accept it except by its installments, hoping to inherit him. So he went to Umar ibn al-Khattab and told him. He sent to his master, but he refused to take it. So Umar said: Take it, O Yarfa, and cast it into the treasury, and give him his installments. And he said: Go, O slave, for you are freed. When the slave's master saw that, he accepted the wealth."
"15254","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, who said: A slave contracted for four thousand or five thousand, and said: Take it all together and leave me be. But his master refused except to take it every year as an installment, hoping to inherit him. So he went to Uthman ibn Affan and mentioned that to him. Uthman called him and offered him to accept it from the slave, but he refused. So he said to the slave: Bring me what is upon you. He brought it to him, and he placed it in the treasury, and wrote him a manumission, and said to the master: Come to me every year and take an installment. When he saw that, he took all his wealth and wrote his manumission."
"15255","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Ata told me: A mukatab offered his master the remainder of his kitaba, but his master refused. So Amr ibn Sa'id, who was the governor of Mecca, said to him: Bring what remains upon you and place it in the treasury; you are free, and you take your installments every year. When his master saw that, he took his wealth. Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Ibn Musafi' told me that he judged likewise in this case concerning Wardan."
"15256","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: Zayd ibn Thabit said: The mukatab is a slave while a dirham remains upon him. And Jabir ibn Abd Allah said: Their stipulations stand among them."
"15257","From Ikrima ibn Ammar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, that Ibn Abbas said: When five uqiyas, or five camels, or five wasqs remain upon the mukatab, then he is a debtor."
"15258","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Abu al-Zubayr told me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying concerning the mukatab who pays the beginning of his kitaba then fails: Is he returned a slave? He said: His master has most right to his stipulation which he stipulated."
"15259","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: He is a slave while anything remains upon him, when he stipulated that upon him."
"15260","Al-Thawri reported from Tariq ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Sha'bi, that Ali said concerning the mukatab who fails: he said: He is freed by account. And Zayd said: He is a slave while a dirham remains upon him. And Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud said: When he pays a third, then he is a debtor."
"15261","Ma'mar reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muslim ibn Jundub, from Ibn Umar, who said: He is a slave while two dirhams remain upon him, meaning the mukatab."
"15262","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar contracted a youth of his. He came to him and said: I have failed. He said: Then erase your kitaba. He said: So he erased it, and Ibn Umar freed him afterwards. He said: Then another youth of his came to him, called Abu Atika, and said: I have failed. He said: Perhaps you want me to free you as I freed your companion. He said: No, but I have failed. He said: So Ibn Umar swore that if he erases his kitaba, he would never free him. He said: So the slave erased it. He said: Then he saw a daughter of his after that and said: Who is this? They said: The daughter of Abu Atika. So he said to Safiyya: What did you say concerning these? She said: You swore not to free them. He said: Then she is free as expiation of my oath; then he freed them."
"15263","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Isma'il ibn Umayya told me that Nafi' told him that Ibn Umar contracted this youth for thirty thousand. He paid fifteen thousand, then came to him and said: I have failed. He said: Then erase it yourself. Nafi' said: So I advised him: Erase it, while he hoped he would free him; so the slave erased it, and he had two daughters and a son. So Ibn Umar said: I keep away my two slave girls. He said: So Ibn Umar freed his son afterwards, then the two slave girls, then him, then he said: Now it is beloved, if you wish. Ibn Jurayj said: I said to Isma'il: What if my mukatab dies a natural death leaving sons born after the kitaba? He said: Nafi' said: His sons are slaves, and his master takes what he left. He said: He did not explain anything in it, but the matter with us is that his sons are upon their father's kitaba."
"15264","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq told me that Zayd ibn Thabit, Ibn Umar, and A'isha used to say: The mukatab is a slave while a dirham remains upon him. Then Zayd contended with them that the mukatab enters upon the Mothers of the Believers while anything remains upon him."
"15265","Ibn Jurayj said: And I was told that Uthman judged that he is a slave while anything remains upon him."
"15266","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, that A'isha said: He is a slave while a dirham remains upon him."
"15267","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Maymun ibn Mihran, that A'isha said to a mukatab of the people of al-Jazira called Humran: Come in to me, even if ten dirhams remain upon you."
"15268","From Abu Ma'shar, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Umm Salama, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The mukatab is a slave while a dirham remains upon him."
"15269","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Nabhan, the mukatab of Umm Salama, told me, saying: I was leading her, I think he said, at al-Bayda, and she said: Who is this? I said: I am Nabhan. She said: I have left the remainder of your kitaba to my nephew Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Umayya, helping him with it in his marriage. He said: I said: I will never pay it to him. She said: If it is only that you may see me and enter upon me, then by Allah you shall never see me ever; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When the mukatab has what he may pay, then veil yourselves from him."
"15270","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: He is a slave while a dirham remains upon him. And Qatada said it."
"15271","Al-Zuhri said: The mukatab, his divorce, his wound, his testimony, and his debt are at the station of the slave. And Qatada said it."
"15272","From Umar ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrima, that Ibn Abbas told him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The blood-money of the mukatab is the blood-money of the freeman in the measure freed of him, and the blood-money of the slave in the measure enslaved of him. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, who said: I was told from him that he used to say concerning the mukatab of Umm Salama: His name is Nufay'. Then he mentioned the like of Ma'mar's hadith."
"15273","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Yahya ibn Sa'id told me that he heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab saying: The mukatab is a slave while a dirham remains upon him."
"15274","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, that Ali said concerning the mukatab: He is inherited in the measure he paid, and he is flogged the hadd in the measure he paid, and he is freed in the measure he paid, and his blood-money is in the measure he paid. And Zayd ibn Thabit said: He is a slave while a dirham remains upon him."
"15275","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was told from Ata al-Khurasani, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever contracts a mukatab for one hundred dirhams, and he pays all of it except ten dirhams, then he is a slave; or for one hundred uqiyas and he pays all of it except an uqiya, then he is a slave."
"15276","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir ibn Samura, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: When the mukatab pays all but half, then no slavery is upon him."
"15277","From Ibn Uyayna, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Amir, that Shurayh used to say: When the mukatab pays his value, then he is a debtor. Al-Sha'bi said: So he used to rule in it by the saying of Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud."
"15278","And as for al-Thawri, he mentioned from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, that Ibn Mas'ud and Shurayh used to say: When he pays a third, then he is a debtor."
"15279","Al-Thawri said: As for Mughira, he told me from Ibrahim that Ibn Mas'ud said: When he pays the worth of his price, then he is a debtor."
"15280","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulayka saying: Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan wrote to Ibn Alqama: When the mukatab pays half his kitaba, then he is a debtor among the debtors, bound by the stipulation. He said: Then it was pressed upon Nafi' ibn Alqama to refer back to him that they would then scheme and make excuses. He said: So he did. He said: So Abd al-Malik wrote: You know best what benefits you, so keep to what benefits you. From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was told from Ata al-Khurasani, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to the people of Mecca, then he mentioned the like of the first hadith."
"15281","Ma'mar reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Salim, freedman of Daws, who said: A'isha said: You are a slave while anything of your kitaba remains upon you."
"15282","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ikrima, that Ali said: The mukatab is freed of him in the measure he paid."
"15283","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid or another, who said: The slaves used to enter upon the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"15284","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: What if the mukatab pays all but one hundred dirhams, does he return a slave? He said: They have claimed that Nafi', freedman of Ibn Umar, reports that from Ibn Umar; had Ibn Umar not said that, we would have followed him. He said: As for me, my view, and it has not reached me from anyone, is that if he fails to pay a small thing of his kitaba, he is not returned a slave, or he would be granted respite for two years and put to work. I said: Then he failed. He said: Then I do not see that he returns a slave. I said: What if a third remains? He said: No. I said: Then a quarter? He said: Yes; when part of a quarter remains, then he does not return a slave. I said: What if he fails in what you deem that if he fails in it he returns a slave, and I had not stipulated that if you fail you return a slave? He said: Then how can he not be a slave while anything remains upon him, when it was stipulated upon him that he is a slave until he takes possession; he is indeed his slave while anything remains upon him."
"15285","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, who said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyab about the mukatab who dies owing a debt. He said: I heard nothing in it. He says: Shurayh used to say: His master shares with them. Ibn al-Musayyab said: Shurayh erred though he was a judge; Zayd ibn Thabit judged that the debt has most right. Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri that he used to say in it the like of Zayd's saying."
"15286","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq told me: I was told from Zayd ibn Thabit that he said concerning the mukatab: His master does not share with the creditors; begin with what began for them before his master's kitaba."
"15287","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: I said to Ata: My mukatab is bankrupt for an installment of his installments that fell due, for he owned his labor in his year. He said: No. And Amr ibn Dinar said. Ibn Jurayj said: I said to Ata: I settled with him upon wealth, and I freed him, and I wrote his settlement upon him as a debt. He said: You do not share with them. And Amr ibn Dinar said it. I said to Ata: It, my ownership of his neck, is gone from me, and I have freed him. He said: If you wish you free him, and if you wish you do not."
"15288","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur, from Ibrahim, concerning the mukatab when he dies owing a debt: he said: His master strikes with what fell due of his installments alongside the creditors. Al-Thawri reported, saying: And al-Shaybani told me, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, the like of it."
"15289","From Abu Sufyan, who said: Ibn Abi Layla, Sufyan al-Thawri, and al-Hasan ibn Salih used to say: When the mukatab dies owing a debt, what is upon him of his kitaba falls due, and the master shares with the creditors for all what is upon him of the kitaba. He said: And Abu Hanifa said: His master has no debt upon him; it belongs to the creditors."
"15290","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: I said to Ata: I wrote upon two men in a sale that your living one guarantees your dead one, and your solvent one guarantees your insolvent one. He said: It is valid. And Amr ibn Dinar said it."
"15291","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: I contracted two slaves of mine and wrote that upon them jointly. He said: It is not valid for your two slaves. And Sulayman ibn Musa said it. I said to Ata: Why is it not valid? He said: Because if one of them went bankrupt, he would return as your slave, and nothing of yours would perish; so for what does this one guarantee to you from him, while the slave is yours? If he dies and you find wealth, you take it; and if you find no wealth for him, he does not guarantee to you from him. From Ibn Jurayj: Because there was no commodity that left you involving wealth. From Ma'mar, from Qatada, who said: It is valid upon them both."
"15292","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: A man said to me: Contract this youth of yours, and his kitaba is upon me. So I did; then he died or failed. He said: He does not guarantee to you from him. He said the like of his saying concerning the two slaves."
"15293","From Ibn Jurayj, and Ata, and Umar, who said: If a man bears for your slave in his kitaba, and you stipulated that if you fail then you are my slave, and a man bore to you for him with his kitaba: they both said: If he fails, then he is your slave, returned, and the man bears nothing from him. And if you did not stipulate that if you fail then you are a slave, they both said: If he fails, you take from the one who bore to you for him your kitaba, and the other hires him out until he recovers what is his. And they both said: If a slave bore to you for him, then your slave died, the other does not guarantee from him, because he died."
"15294","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who says to a man: Contract this slave of yours; if he fails, his kitaba is upon me. He said: It is valid. Ma'mar said: As for the people of Kufa, they do not deem it anything; among them Hammad and Ibn Shubruma and others say: Your wealth guaranteed you from your wealth."
"15295","From al-Thawri, who said: The mukatab, if his master guarantees his kitaba, it is nothing; this is no surety, for he is his slave."
"15296","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: If a man says to a man: Free this youth of yours, and his price is upon me. He said: This is valid, and his wala is for his master as he freed him, and upon the bearer is what he bore."
"15297","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Hafsa contracted a youth of hers for servants. Nafi' said: I saw some of them."
"15298","From Hushaym ibn Bashir, who said: A shaykh of Banu Sulaym called Abd al-Hamid ibn Sawwar told me, saying: A sister-in-law of mine who was a freedwoman of Abu Barza al-Aslami, called Sara, told me, from Abu Barza al-Aslami, that he contracted a youth for slaves."
"15299","From Hushaym, from al-Hajjaj, who said: Ibn Abi Mulayka told us that a man contracted a youth of his for ten thousand dirhams and for a youth practicing the like of his craft. He said: So the youth paid the wealth by its installments upon which he contracted, but he found no youth practicing the like of his craft. So he disputed with him to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. Umar said to him: Give him a youth practicing the like of your craft. He said: I cannot find him. He said: Seek him. He said: I sought him and did not find him. He said: So Umar returned him to slavery."
"15300","From Hushaym, or another, from Ibrahim, who said: There is no harm in a man contracting his slave for servants, and marrying for servants. He said: And Ibn al-Taymi told us, from Abu Awana, from al-Mughira, from Ibrahim, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, the like of it."
"15301","From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, that Salman al-Farisi contracted upon planting one hundred young palm shoots, and when they bear fruit, then he is free."
"15302","From Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya, who said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad told me, from his father, that Salman al-Farisi belonged to some people of Banu al-Nadir. They contracted him upon planting for them such-and-such young shoots, until it reaches ten fronds. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Place a young shoot at each poor spot. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came in the morning and placed them with his hand, and prayed for him in them; it was as if upon the swell of the sea. I held back one young shoot from them. When Allah granted it to him as fay, and it is al-Muthib, Allah made it charity, so it is a charity in Medina. From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, who said: I heard Salman mentioning that a dozen-odd masters passed him from master to master."
"15303","From Ma'mar, from a man of some of his companions, who said: Some people visited Salman while he was governor at al-Mada'in, and he was working this palm-leaf. It was said to him: Do you work this while you are a governor, and a provision is paid to you? He said: I love to eat from the work of my hand; and I shall tell you how I learned this. I was among my family at Ramhurmuz, and I used to go to my schoolteacher, and on the road was a monk. Whenever I passed, I sat with him, and he used to tell me of the tidings of the heaven and the earth and the like of that, until I was distracted from my schooling and kept to him. The teacher told my family, saying: This monk has corrupted your son. He said: So they drove him out, and I hid from them. He said: So I left with him until we came to al-Mawsil, where we found forty monks, and they held the monk with whom I had come in immense veneration. I was with them for months, then I fell ill. One of the monks said: I am going to Bayt al-Maqdis to pray in it. I rejoiced at that and said: I am with you. He said: So we left. He said: I never saw anyone more enduring in walking than him; he would walk, and when he saw me exhausted, he would say: Sleep, and he would stand praying. He remained so, not eating for a day, until we came to Bayt al-Maqdis. When we reached it, he slept and said to me: When you see the shadow here, wake me. When the shadow reached that place, I wanted to wake him, then I said: A month and he did not sleep; by Allah, I shall leave him a little. So I left him a while. He awoke and saw the shadow had passed that place, and said: Did I not tell you to wake me? I said: You had not slept, so I loved to let you sleep a little. He said: I do not love that an hour passes upon me but that I am remembering Allah Almighty in it. He said: Then we entered Bayt al-Maqdis, and there was a crippled beggar asking. He asked him, and I do not know what he said to him. The crippled one said to him: You entered and gave me nothing, and you left and gave me nothing. He said: Would you love to stand? He said: So he prayed for him and he stood. I began to marvel and follow him, then I was distracted, and the monk went away. Then I went out following him, asking about him. I saw a company of the Ansar and asked them about him, saying: Have you seen a man of such-and-such description? They said: This is a runaway slave. They seized me and mounted me behind one of them, until they brought me to Medina. They placed me in a garden of theirs, and I used to work this palm-leaf; thence I learned it. He said: And the monk had said: Allah Almighty has not given the Arabs any of the prophets, and a prophet shall come forth from them; if you reach him, believe him and have faith in him. His sign is that he accepts the gift and does not eat charity, and upon his back is the seal of prophethood. He said: So I stayed as I stayed. Then they said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has come to Medina. I went out with some dates I had, and brought them to him, saying: What is this? I said: Charity. He said: We do not eat charity. So I took it. Then I came to him with dates and placed them before him. He said: What is this? I said: A gift. He ate, and those with him ate. Then I stood behind him to look at the seal. He noticed me and cast his cloak from his shoulders, and I believed in him and affirmed him. He said: Then he either contracted upon one hundred palm trees, or he bought himself with one hundred palm trees. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) planted them with his hand, and a year had not passed before they bore fruit, or he said: He ate from them."
"15304","Ma'mar reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, who said: A man died leaving a mukatab who had paid part of his kitaba. His sons inherited him. Then the mukatab died leaving wealth. Ibn al-Musayyab and Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman were asked about him, and they both said: What remains of his kitaba is shared among the sons of his master, the men and the women, upon their inheritance; and what is surplus of the wealth after his kitaba is for the males among them, not the females."
"15305","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: His wala belongs to the asaba of the one who contracted him."
"15306","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who contracted a slave of his, then the master died leaving men and women: he said: The women have nothing of the wala of the mukatab; what he pays follows the inheritance among them, and the wala is for the males."
"15307","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: The woman inherits nothing of the wala, unless she herself frees him, then his wala is hers; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The wala belongs to the one who freed."
"15308","From al-Thawri, concerning a woman who inherited a mukatab of hers from her father, she and her brother, and both freed the mukatab: he said: The wala belongs to the brother; she inherited only dirhams. He said: And we hold that. He said: And had the woman freed her share of the mukatab, there would be no liability upon her; and if he fails, he is returned into slavery, for she only left dirhams, and there becomes for her a share of that mukatab; her freeing does not avail."
"15309","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: A woman inherited her father as a mukatab; he paid his installments until he was freed, then the mukatab died while the woman who came to own him is alive. He said: Then she does not inherit him, but his asaba inherit him. And Amr ibn Dinar said it, meaning the asaba of her father. And Amr said to me: Judgment never ceased so, and it is judged that the woman does not inherit the wala of her husband's mukatabs, even if they pass to her."
"15310","Ata said: Whoever inherits a mukatab, and the mukatab fails and returns a slave, then he is a slave of the one who inherited him, upon his stipulation upon which he contracted him."
"15311","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Ibn Umar asked Zayd ibn Thabit about a freedman of Umar who died: Do the daughters of Umar inherit? So Zayd said: ... to you ... that he should let them inherit."
"15312","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, concerning a man and a woman who inherited a mukatab, and he paid them both: he said: His wala belongs to them both. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, the like of it. He said: And his father used to say: I never thought anyone of the people would differ in that, and I marvel at their saying: She has no wala."
"15313","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: His wala belongs to the man, not the woman."
"15314","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, and from Ibn Tawus, from his father, concerning a man who contracted a slave, then died leaving two sons of his, and the mukatab fell to one of them, and he paid until he was freed: they both said: His wala belongs to them both upon their inheritance portions from their father, for he was freed under their father's kitaba, unless one of them frees him, then his wala belongs to the one who freed him."
"15315","From Ma'mar, who said: I said to Ibn Tawus: What if one owns ten and one owns one, is it halves? He said: My father used to say: It is among them over eleven shares, meaning the wala."
"15316","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: A man died leaving two sons of his, and leaving the mukatab; the mukatab fell to one of them, then he paid his kitaba until he was freed, then the mukatab died leaving wealth, while both sons of his master are alive, the one to whom he fell in inheritance and the other; who inherits him? He said: They both inherit him together. And Amr ibn Dinar said it. Ata said: His wala returned to the one who contracted him. I said to Ata: What if the one who inherited him from his father freed him outright, taking nothing from him? He said: Then his wala belongs to the one who freed him. I said: What if the one who inherited him took something from him and freed him? He said: If he took something from him as compensation for him, then freed him, his wala belongs to their father who contracted him. If he took from him a small thing with no compensation, then freed him, his wala belongs to the one who freed him; he affirmed this to me many times over that period. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: If he took from him a compensation, and something of it remained upon him, then freed him, his wala belongs to the one who inherited him, the one who freed him, for he is a slave while anything remains; if he fails for a little of his kitaba, he returns a slave."
"15317","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu al-Zubayr told me that in the report of Urwa to him, from Barira: That she belonged to some people of Banu Amir ibn Sa'sa'a, and her mukatab contracted upon nine uqiyas. They sold her to A'isha with her kitaba as it was, and she had paid nothing of her kitaba."
"15318","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab said concerning a woman who died leaving a mukatab with none of his installments fallen due, and her husband and her son inherited her: He paid his kitaba and they both freed him together. He said: If he pays his kitaba and they do not free him, his wala belongs to the one who contracted him; and if they freed him, the wala is theirs. For it reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The wala belongs to the one who freed."
"15319","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: A youth I contracted, then I sold his neck and his kitaba to a man, and he failed. He said: Then he is a slave of the one who bought him. And Amr ibn Dinar said. I said to Ata: Then he paid and was freed. He said: Then he is his freedman; he belongs to the one who bought him. He said: Then how, when kitaba is but manumission? He said: No, it is not manumission; that is only said of the mukatab who is inherited by succession, of whom it is said: If a man inherits him, he may not sell him except with the permission of the asaba of the one who contracted him. And Amr ibn Dinar said. I said: Is this an opinion? He said: Yes. Ata said: Unless he sells what is upon him outright; if he fails, he is a slave of the one who inherited him, the one who sold him, and this one sells him for his debt for which he bought what is upon him; and if he frees, his wala belongs to the one who inherited him, the one who sold what is upon him; he is a slave sold for a debt upon him. I said to Ata: Does it suffice me that my brother of Banu Abi gives me permission to sell him that day, while the freedmen of my father did not give me permission? He said: Yes; it suffices you that his heirs of his asaba that day give you permission."
"15320","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: As for a mukatab you contracted, then you sold his neck and what is upon him, you need no one's permission in it; if he fails, he belongs to the one who bought him, and if he frees him, he is his freedman. He said: And I say: No."
"15321","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, concerning a people who inherited a mukatab, men and women, and they freed him: they said: He is freed, and his wala belongs to them upon their portions, for the men and the women."
"15322","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Ata said to me: Whose debt is sold upon him, he has most right to it, taking it for the price if he wishes."
"15323","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Hasan ibn Muslim said: It reached me that the mukatab is sold, and he has most right to himself, taking himself for what he was sold for. And in the Book of Sales is clarification of that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz."
"15324","From Ma'mar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, that whose debt is sold upon him, he is most entitled to it. Ma'mar said: As for the people of Kufa, they do not deem it anything."
"15325","Ma'mar reported from a man of Quraysh, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz forbade concerning a mukatab who bought what was upon him with goods, making the mukatab most entitled to himself. Then he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Whoever buys a debt upon a man, the debtor is most entitled to it when he pays the like of what his fellow paid."
"15326","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: I never saw the judges but judging: Whoever buys a debt upon a man, the debtor is most entitled to it."
"15327","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: I said to Ata: What if my mukatab fails, how about what you know the people gave him? He said: Most beloved to me is that he withhold it from him in that case; and if he keeps it, there is no harm."
"15328","From Ma'mar, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, concerning the mukatab who fails and returns a slave, while the people had given him something: he said: He puts what the people gave him into freeing necks. From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, the like of it."
"15329","From Ibn Uyayna, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, that he was asked about a man who bought an insane youth and freed him unknowingly: he said: There is refunded to him the difference between soundness and insanity, then he puts it into a neck or gives it in charity."
"15330","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Ata said to me concerning the mukatab whom his master permits to marry: His master then owns no right to separate between them."
"15331","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Ata said to me: The mukatab is sold only for goods. And Ata before this, and it was his first saying: The mukatab is not sold. And Ibn Mas'ud used to dislike the sale of the mukatab."
"15332","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, who said: Ibn Umar used to forbid settling with the mukatabs except for goods. Al-Zuhri said: And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to that effect."
"15333","From Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya, who said: A shaykh of the people of Medina told me that Umm Salama, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), settled with a mukatab of hers called Nassah for gold or silver."
"15334","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: We know no harm in it, and we know none disliked it except Ibn Umar."
"15335","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, that he was asked about the mukatab for whom reduction is made and hastening taken from him, and he saw no harm in it; and Ibn Umar disliked it except for goods."
"15336","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: And I say: There is no harm in selling the mukatab for goods."
"15337","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, who said: Hasan ibn Muslim said to me, while we were with Ibn Tawus: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote forbidding settling with the mukatabs except for goods. And this one sees no harm in it, pointing to Tawus. He said: So I said: Glory be to Allah! After the word of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz? He said: So Tawus heard me and said: Whence are you? I said: Of the people of Iraq. He said: You deem that there is none shrewder than you."
"15338","From Ibrahim ibn Umar, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayya, that Ibrahim, al-Hasan, and Ibn Sirin disliked settling with the mukatabs except for goods."
"15339","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who has intercourse with his female mukatab: he said: He is flogged one hundred; if she bears a child, she becomes of the mothers of children. Ma'mar said: And some of the people of Medina said: She is given the choice; if she wishes, she becomes of the mothers of children, and if she wishes, she stays upon her kitaba, and the child is attached to him."
"15340","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, who said: He is flogged one hundred minus a lash, and he pays her bride-price if he coerced her; and if he did not coerce her, then nothing; and her bride-price is the mahr of her like. Ma'mar said: And Qatada said: And if she obeyed him, she too is flogged; and if he coerced her, there is no flogging upon her."
"15341","From al-Thawri, concerning the one who frequents his female mukatab: he said: She gets the dower, and the hadd is averted from her, whether he coerced her or she obeyed him. The female mukatab who bears is given the choice: if she wishes, she becomes an umm walad and leaves her kitaba; and if she wishes, she pays her kitaba and does not become an umm walad. If she chooses to be a mukataba, then he dies before she pays her kitaba, she is freed."
"15342","From Ibn Abi Sabra, from Abu al-Zinad and Yahya ibn Sa'id, who both said: Concerning the man who has intercourse with his female mukatab: If she obeyed him, both are flogged and she gets nothing; and if he coerced her, he is flogged and pays her the like of the dower of her like. If she bears, she becomes an umm walad and her kitaba is void."
"15343","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, who said: When two mukatabs, each of them buys his fellow from his master, this one this one from his master and this one this one from his master, then the sale belongs to the first. Ma'mar said: And I heard one who says of the people of Medina: The wala belongs to the buying master; they say: He only bought what is upon the mukatab, so the wala belongs to the master."
"15344","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no vow in disobedience, nor in what the son of Adam does not own."
"15345","Ma'mar reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Qilaba, from Thabit ibn al-Dahhak, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no vow in what you do not own."
"15346","From Ma'mar, from Zayd ibn Rufay', from Abu Ubayda ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: The vow neither advances anything nor delays it; but Allah Almighty extracts thereby from the miser. There is no fulfilling a vow in disobedience of Allah, and its expiation is the expiation of an oath."
"15347","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no fulfilling a vow in disobedience of Allah, nor in what the son of Adam does not own."
"15348","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from a man of Banu Hanifa, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no vow in anger nor in disobedience of Allah, and its expiation is the expiation of an oath. As for Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of this."
"15349","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Hasan ibn Muslim told me that a man vowed at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to fast and to stand in the sun praying, and not to speak to the people. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he called him and said to him: Did you vow not to speak to the people? Then speak to the people! And to stand in the sun praying? Then seek shade! And you vowed to fast? Then fast! He said: And Tawus used to call him Abu Isra'il."
"15350","And that a woman came with a husband of hers; the enemy seized her husband and bound him; and she was upon the mount of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She vowed that if she reaches Medina, she would slaughter it. When she came, she told the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of her vow. He said: Evil is what you rewarded your she-camel with; do not slaughter it, for you do not own it."
"15351","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Abu Isra'il while he was standing in the sun. He asked about him, and it was said: He vowed to stand in the sun, to fast, and not to speak. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Proceed with your fast, remember Allah, and sit in the shade."
"15352","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque, while Abu Isra'il was praying. It was said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Here he is, O Messenger of Allah; he neither sits, nor speaks to the people, nor seeks shade, intending fasting. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let him sit, speak to the people, fast, and seek shade. And Ibn Tawus said: So I said to him: Then did Abu Isra'il vow to do that? He said: Thus I heard as I was told. Ibn Tawus said: And I heard my father saying since I understood: There is no vow in disobedience of Allah, and no vow in what you do not own."
"15353","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, from Hayyaj, that a youth of his father fled. He made a vow upon himself that if he gains power over him, he would cut off a limb of him. When he gained power over him, he sent me to Imran ibn Husayn and I asked him. Imran said: Order your father to free his youth and expiate his oath; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to urge us to charity and forbid us mutilation. He said: So I went to Samura and asked him, and he said the like of Imran's saying."
"15354","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abida, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by some people and greeted them, but they did not return it upon him, or he said: They did not speak. He asked about them, and it was said: They vowed or swore not to speak today. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ruined are the deep-divers, meaning the hair-splitters; he said it twice."
"15355","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrima, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man standing, I thought he said while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was preaching, and said: What is with this? They said: This is Abu Isra'il; he put a vow upon himself to stand a day in the sun, fasting it, and not speaking. He said: Then let him sit, seek shade, speak, and complete his fast."
"15356","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that a man vowed to give charity to the first man of the villagers he finds, then he gave charity to the first man he saw of the villagers after that. It was said to him: This is the vilest man in the village. Then he gave charity to another man, and it was said to him: He is rich. That grieved him. He saw in a dream that Allah has accepted your charity: That so-and-so woman was a prostitute, and need used to drive her to that; she abandoned that since you gave her your charity and became chaste. And that so-and-so used to steal, and need used to drive him to that; he abandoned that since you gave him, and desisted from theft. And that so-and-so was rich and never gave charity; when you gave him charity, he said: I am more deserving of charity than this one, and wealthier; so Allah opened for him charity."
"15357","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Abu al-Zubayr told me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: There is no fulfilling a vow in disobedience of Allah."
"15358","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Abi Husayn told me, saying: A man came to Ibn Abbas and said: I vowed to bare myself a day till night upon Hira. Ibn Abbas said: Satan only wanted to disgrace you. Then he recited: O Children of Adam, let not Satan tempt you, the verse. Perform ablution, then wear your garment, and pray upon Hira a day till night."
"15359","Ibn Jurayj said: And one of our companions told me that Ibn al-Zubayr deemed vows fulfilled. A man came to Ibn Abbas and said: I vowed to carry a pillar of the pillars of the mosque. He said: Then go to Ibn al-Zubayr, let him order you to carry a pillar of the pillars of the mosque."
"15360","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar mentioning that a woman came to Mu'awiya during some hajj or umra of his, and said: I vowed not to strike my head with a khimar. He said: Go and ask, then come and tell me. She came to Ibn Abbas, and he said: Wear the khimar. She told Mu'awiya what he said, and it pleased him."
"15361","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, who said: A man asked Ibn al-Musayyab about a man who vowed a vow not befitting him; he mentioned that it is disobedience, and he ordered him to fulfill it. He said: Then the man asked Ikrima, and he ordered him to expiate his oath and not fulfill his vow. He said: So the man returned to Ibn al-Musayyab and told him Ikrima's saying. Ibn al-Musayyab said: Ikrima must surely stop, or his back will surely be hurt. The man returned to Ikrima and told him. Ikrima said to him: Since you conveyed to me, then convey to him: As for him, the rulers struck his back, and stood him in a hair garment; ask him about your vow: Is it obedience to Allah or disobedience? If he says: It is disobedience, then he has ordered you to disobey Allah; and if he says: It is obedience to Allah, then he has lied about Allah when he claimed that disobedience of Allah is obedience."
"15362","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: The vow has only fulfillment. Ma'mar reported from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyab, the like of Ibn Umar's saying."
"15363","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man standing in the sun. He asked about him, and they said: He is devout. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Remember Allah."
"15364","Ma'mar reported, saying: I asked al-Zuhri about the vow one vows. He said: If it is obedience to Allah, he must fulfill it; and if it is disobedience to Allah, let him draw near to Allah with what he wishes."
"15365","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: When one vows to hajj or umra, or to free, or vows some good in thanks he thanks Allah for, let him execute it; and if it is an oath, let him expiate his oath, like his saying: If Allah saves me from this pain, if Allah saves me from the robbers."
"15366","From Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya, from Isma'il ibn Abi Uwaymir, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The vow is upon four aspects: A vow in what he cannot bear, in it is the expiation of an oath; and a vow in disobedience of Allah, its expiation is the expiation of an oath; and a vow he did not name, its expiation is the expiation of an oath; and a vow in obedience of Allah Almighty, its maker should fulfill it."
"15367","From al-Thawri, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Jabir ibn Zayd, concerning a man who put a vow upon himself: he said: If he intended, it is what he intended; and if he named, it is what he named; and if he neither intended nor named, then if he wishes he fasts a day, and if he wishes he feeds a poor one, and if he wishes he prays two rak'as."
"15368","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, concerning the vow and the unlawful: When he names nothing, he said: The gravest oath; upon him is a neck to free, or fasting two consecutive months, or feeding sixty poor."
"15369","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, concerning one who says: All lawful is unlawful upon me, it is an oath. He said: And Qatada used to rule by it. Ma'mar said: As for Amr ibn Ubayd, he told me from al-Hasan that he said: What he intended. From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: What he intended."
"15370","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The vow whose maker does not name it, it is the gravest of oaths, and it has the gravest expiation: freeing a neck."
"15371","From al-Thawri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Ma'shar, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Umar, that he was asked about vows, and he said: It is the best of oaths; if he cannot afford then the next, and if he cannot then the next, meaning: freeing a slave, clothing, and feeding."
"15372","From al-Thawri, from Abu Khalid, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: A vow, its expiation is the expiation of an oath."
"15373","Al-Thawri also mentioned it, from al-Hajjaj, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Sadusi narrated to me, that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: Concerning a vow there is expiation of an oath."
"15374","From al-Thawri, from Hushaym, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Concerning a vow there is expiation of an oath."
"15375","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, he said: I heard al-Sha'bi saying: I am amazed at one who says: A vow is an emphasized oath."
"15376","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, and Khalid, from al-Hasan, they both said: A vow is an oath: feeding ten poor persons."
"15377","Al-Thawri said, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Fasting three days suffices him for a vow."
"15378","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: A vow is an oath."
"15379","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abd Allah ibn Murrah, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade us from vows, and said: It does not bring anything forward, it is only extracted thereby from the miserly."
"15380","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Ajlan, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: I will never make a vow, and I will never observe i'tikaf, and he mentioned the third and I forgot it."
"15381","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: A man vowed that he would not eat with the orphan sons of his brother, so Umar ibn al-Khattab was informed, and he said: Go and eat with them, so he did."
"15382","From al-Thawri, he said: If he says: a vow solemnly bound, or an obligatory vow, or a vow in which there is no expiation, then it is a vow, and the word is decisive."
"15383","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from al-Hasan, concerning a man who says: upon me is a vow or a sacrificial offering, without naming anything, he said: Expiation of an oath."
"15384","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Awf ibn al-Harith, he being the maternal nephew of A'ishah, that A'ishah narrated to him, that Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr said concerning a sale or a gift she had given: By Allah, A'ishah must stop or I will place her under interdiction. A'ishah said: Did he say this. They said: Yes. So A'ishah said: It is upon me as a vow to Allah that I will never speak a word to Ibn al-Zubayr. He said: So Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr sought intercession to her when her boycott of him grew long, and she said: By Allah, I will not allow anyone to intercede regarding him. When that weighed long upon Ibn al-Zubayr, he spoke to al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad ibn Abd Yaghuth, they being from Banu Zuhrah, and said to them: I adjure you by Allah but that you take me in to A'ishah, for it is not lawful for her to vow to sever ties with me. So al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Abd al-Rahman came, with Ibn al-Zubayr wrapped in their cloaks, until they sought permission from A'ishah, and said: Peace upon the Prophet and mercy of Allah, may we enter. A'ishah said: Enter. They said: All of us, O Mother of the Believers. She said: Yes, enter all of you, while A'ishah did not know that Ibn al-Zubayr was with them. When they entered, Ibn al-Zubayr rushed through the curtain, embraced A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and began pleading with her and weeping, and al-Miswar and Abd al-Rahman began pleading with A'ishah to speak to him and accept from him, saying to her: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade what you did of boycotting, for it is not lawful for a Muslim to boycott his brother for more than three nights. When they pressed A'ishah with reminding and reproaching, she began reminding them and weeping, saying: I have made a vow, and a vow is grave. They kept with her until she spoke to Ibn al-Zubayr. Then she freed forty slaves for that vow of hers. Then she would remember that vow of hers after she had freed forty, then weep until her tears wet her headcover."
"15385","From Ma'mar, he said: One who heard al-Hasan informed me, he saying concerning a man who vowed without naming anything, he said: It is an oath, he expiates it. Ma'mar said: And Qatadah said: It is an emphasized oath: freeing a slave, or fasting two months, or feeding sixty poor persons."
"15386","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is people's saying: upon me is a vow to Allah. He said: It is an oath; if he names that vow of his, then it is what he named. He said: And I asked him about a man's saying: upon me is a vow whose expiation is only fulfilling it. He said: It is an oath so long as he does not name it."
"15387","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata informed me, that he heard Abu al-Sha'tha saying: If a man vows he will surely do something, then it is like an oath so long as he does not name the vow."
"15388","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: If he says: upon me is a vow, or says: upon me to Allah is a vow, then it is an oath."
"15389","Ma'mar informed us, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about a man who says: upon me is a vow, he said: I do not know what this is. He said: And al-Hasan and Qatadah used to say: It is an oath. Qatadah said: An emphasized oath."
"15390","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: If a man vows good, let him carry it out, meaning: if he imposes upon himself fasting, or whatever good it be, let him carry it out."
"15391","I said: If he says: if Allah, mighty and majestic, heals me, then upon me is fasting or walking, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman used to say: Let him carry it out, it is not an oath. Ibn Tawus said: My father said: If he says: if I do not do such and such, then upon me is fasting, upon me is walking, upon me is prayer, upon me is a sacrificial offering, then it is an oath among oaths."
"15392","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: A man came to Ibn Abbas and said: My father was captured by the Daylam, and I vowed that if Allah saved him I would stand naked upon a mountain, I think he said: upon Uhud, and fast a day. He said: What if Iblis musters his troops against you and says: Look at this son of Adam, how I have mocked him, or a wind comes and throws you down and you die, would you consider yourself a martyr. He said: What do you think. He said: Wear your clothes, fast a day, and pray standing and sitting."
"15393","Ma'mar informed us, he said: One who was with al-Hasan when a man came to him informed me, and said: O Abu Sa'id, a woman vowed to pray two rak'ahs behind every pillar in the mosque, and she has prayed at every pillar in the mosque except this pillar of yours. He said: If she had gathered that behind one pillar, it would have sufficed her. Then he moved away from that pillar for her until she prayed."
"15394","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, and from Layth, from Tawus, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: No nose-rein, no halter, no wandering, and Ibn Jurayj added: No celibacy, no monasticism in Islam."
"15395","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Sulayman al-Ahwal informed me, that Tawus informed him, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed while circumambulating the Ka'bah by a man leading a man by a nose-rein in his nose, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, cut it with his hand, then ordered him to lead him by his hand."
"15396","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Sulayman al-Ahwal informed me, that Tawus informed him, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed while circumambulating the Ka'bah by a man who had tied his hand to another man with a strap, or a thread, or something else, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, cut it, then said: Lead him by his hand."
"15397","Ibn Jurayj informed us, that a man came to Ibn Umar and said: I vowed I would walk to Makkah, but I could not. He said: Walk what you can and ride, until when you enter the sanctuary then walk until you enter, and slaughter, or give charity."
"15398","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by a woman with her hair uncovered, barefoot, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered her to cover her head and wear sandals, then asked what her matter was. They said: She vowed to walk barefoot with her hair uncovered, so he forbade her."
"15399","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn Abbas, that a man vowed to walk to Makkah, he said: He walks, and when he tires he rides; if it be the next year, he walks what he rode and rides what he walked, and slaughters a camel."
"15400","From al-Thawri, from Mansur and Mughirah, from Ibrahim, the like of that, except that Mughirah said: He offers a sacrificial animal."
"15401","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, and Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Umm Mahabbah, that she vowed to walk to the Ka'bah, so she walked until when she reached the pass of al-Batn she tired, so she rode. Then she came to Ibn Abbas and asked him, and he said to her: Can you perform Hajj next year, and ride until you reach the place from which you rode, then walk what you rode. She said: No. He said: Have you a daughter to walk on your behalf. She said: I have two daughters, but they deem themselves too great for that. He said: Seek forgiveness from Allah Almighty."
"15402","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from al-Hakam, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Whoever vows to perform Hajj walking, let him perform Hajj from Makkah."
"15403","From Abd Allah, from Shu'bah, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from Ali, concerning one who vowed to walk to the House, he said: He walks, and when he tires he rides, and offers a camel."
"15404","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, he said: He walks, and when walking fails him he rides, and offers a camel; if he imposed upon himself to walk barefoot, let him wear sandals or light shoes, and shed blood. He said: And al-Hasan said: And he walks from the land from which he vowed."
"15405","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ubayd Allah ibn Zahr, from Abd Allah ibn Malik, from Abu Sa'id al-Yahsabi, that Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: My sister vowed to walk barefoot uncovered. He said: Order her to ride, to cover her head, and to fast three days, and by this he used to give fatwa."
"15406","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, that Uqbah ibn Amir asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about a sister of his who vowed to walk to the House, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Let her ride. Then he asked him a second time, and he said: Let her ride. Then he asked him, I think he said a third time, and he said: Let her ride, for Allah is free of need of her walking."
"15407","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub informed me, that Yazid ibn Abi Habib informed him, that Abu al-Khayr narrated to him, from Uqbah ibn Amir, that he said: My sister vowed to walk to the House of Allah, mighty and majestic, so she ordered me to seek a fatwa for her from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, so I sought a fatwa for her from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said: Let her walk and let her ride. He said: Abu al-Khayr would not part from Uqbah."
"15408","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata was asked while I listened, about a Ruhatiyyah woman who vowed: if she took maintenance from a brother of hers, she would surely walk on her face to Makkah, so he said: She only vowed disobedience of Allah, so let her come riding, until when she is at the sanctuary, let her enter ihram for Umrah, and walk until she sees the House. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: Let her perform Umrah from Ruhat."
"15409","He said: And Ata was asked about a man who vowed he would surely walk, but did not walk until he grew old and weak, so he said: Let one of his sons walk on his behalf."
"15410","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata asked about a man who vowed he would surely perform Hajj or Umrah walking, without intending in himself from where to walk, he said: Let him walk from his miqat."
"15411","Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, concerning one who vowed to perform Hajj walking, he said: What he intended, and he used to make them walk from al-Basrah."
"15412","Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, concerning one who vowed to walk to Makkah then was unable, he said: He rides and offers a camel."
"15413","Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan concerning a man who says: upon me is walking to the House, he said: It is an oath, he expiates it."
"15414","From Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: Whoever says: upon me is walking to the House of Allah, without saying: upon me is a vow, then it is nothing."
"15415","From Umar ibn Dharr, he said: I asked Mujahid about a man who says: upon me is walking to the House of Allah, without naming from where to walk, he said: He walks, and when unable he rides, and let him enter the sanctuary walking, and let him offer for his riding."
"15416","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Al-Hasan and Jabir ibn Zayd were asked about a man who said: if I do not do such and such, then I am in ihram for Hajj, they said: Ihram is only upon one who intended Hajj, it is an oath he expiates. Ma'mar said: And Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, the like of that."
"15417","From al-Thawri, from a man, from Mujahid, he said: It is nothing, and al-Hasan said: Expiation of an oath, and al-Sha'bi and Ibrahim said: It binds him."
"15418","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from Fudayl, from Ibrahim, from al-Sha'bi, they said: When the months of Hajj enter he enters ihram for Hajj, this one who says: he is in ihram for Hajj."
"15419","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: Whoever says: upon me is a Hajj, or: to Allah upon me is a Hajj, then it is an oath."
"15420","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man vowed he would surely walk to Bayt al-Maqdis from al-Basrah, he said: You were only commanded to this House, so let him walk to this House. He said: And likewise concerning staying in devotion. He said: I said: What about a bequest, a man bequeaths in a matter, then I see better than it. He said: Do what is better, so long as the man does not name anything; but if he says: for the poor, or in the path of Allah, and you see better than that, then do what is better. Then he retracted from that and said: Let him do what he said, and carry out his order. He said: And his first saying is more pleasing to me."
"15421","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata informed me, that A'ishah daughter of Abu Bakr had vowed devotion in the interior of Thabir, and her brother Abd al-Rahman would prevent her until he died, then she stayed in devotion there."
"15422","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me concerning those who vow devotion on mountaintops, he said: Let them stay in devotion at the mosque."
"15423","Ma'mar informed us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: Whoever vows to observe i'tikaf in the mosque of Iliya, then observes i'tikaf in the mosque of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, at Madinah, it suffices him; and whoever vows to observe i'tikaf in the mosque of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then observes i'tikaf in the Sacred Mosque, it suffices him; and whoever vows to observe i'tikaf on mountaintops, then that is not proper for him, let him observe i'tikaf in a congregational mosque."
"15424","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Yusuf ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Sufyan informed me, that Hafs ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf and Amr ibn Hannah informed him, from Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from men of the Ansar among the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that a man of the Ansar came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day of the Conquest, while the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was sitting in a gathering near the Maqam, and greeted the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Prophet of Allah, I vowed that if Allah grants victory to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and to the believers at Makkah, I would surely pray in Bayt al-Maqdis, and I found a man of the people of Sham here among Quraysh as a protector coming with me and going. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Pray here. The man repeated saying it three times, each time the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Pray here. Then he said his saying a fourth time, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Then go and pray in it; by Him who sent Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, if you had prayed here it would have sufficed you for prayer in Bayt al-Maqdis. Ibn Jurayj said: I was informed that that man was al-Sharid ibn Suwayd of al-Sadif, he being among Thaqif."
"15425","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he said: Al-Sharid came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I vowed that if Allah grants you victory I would pray in Bayt al-Maqdis, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Pray here. Then he repeated until he said this saying of his three times, the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Pray here. Then he said to him the fourth time: Go; by Him in whose hand is my soul, if you had prayed here it would have sufficed you. Then he said: Prayer in this Sacred Mosque is better than a hundred thousand prayers."
"15426","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ibn Tawus, he said: Whoever came to my father and said: I vowed walking to Bayt al-Maqdis, or visiting Bayt al-Maqdis, he would say: Upon you is Makkah."
"15427","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man said to Ata: A man assigns a she-camel in the path of Allah, he said: Has he relatives. He said: Yes. He said: Then let him hand it to them. He said: And this was his fatwa in that and its likes."
"15428","Al-Thawri informed us, he said: Ata ibn al-Sa'ib informed me, from Murrah al-Hamdani, he said: I used to pray at every pillar in the mosque two rak'ahs, then a man came to Abd Allah while I was with him, and said: What think you of a man who prays in this mosque at every pillar two rak'ahs. Abd Allah said: If this one knew that Allah is at the first pillar, he would not move until he finished his prayer."
"15429","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: A man vowed to circumambulate on his knees seven times, so he said: Ibn Abbas said: They were not ordered to circumambulate crawling, but let him circumambulate fourteen, seven for his feet and seven for his hands. I said: And he did not order him expiation. He said: No."
"15430","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: So he vowed he would surely circumambulate blindfolded, is he led. He said: Let him not do it, nor expiate. I said: What about a man who vowed he would surely walk in an Umrah with no garment on his back. He said: Let him wear. I said: Or barefoot. He said: Let him wear sandals, then let him slaughter or fast. I said to him: What about a man who vowed he would surely bring his she-camel to visit the House. He said: Let him do it, let him hamstring it, as a pilgrim or an Umrah performer. I repeated it to him and said to him: Do camels visit the House, but he refused but that, twice."
"15431","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about a man who vowed devotion or walking then died without carrying it out, he said: Then his guardian carries it out on his behalf. I said: What about another of his relatives. He said: Yes, and the guardians are most beloved to him."
"15432","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, that Abu al-Sha'tha informed him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was come to by a man whose father or mother had died, upon whom was a vow, he thought he said: a vow or Hajj, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Fulfill it on his behalf."
"15433","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, that Sa'd ibn Ubadah asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about a vow that was upon his mother, so he ordered him to fulfill it."
"15434","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq, he said: I heard Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah mentioning that his mother died while i'tikaf was upon her, he said: So I hastened with my brothers to Ibn Abbas, asked him, and he said: Observe i'tikaf on her behalf and fast."
"15435","From Ma'mar, he said: I heard a man, I thought him of the children of Asma daughter of Abu Bakr, narrating to Hisham ibn Urwah that Asma ordered in her illness that walking that was upon her be fulfilled on her behalf."
"15436","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: Sa'd ibn Ubadah came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: My mother had a vow upon her, shall I fulfill it. He said: Yes. He said: Will that benefit her. He said: Yes."
"15437","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed me, he said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad saying: A woman asked Ibn Abbas about a man who vowed to slaughter his son at the Ka'bah, he said: Then let him not slaughter his son, and let him expiate for his oath. A man said to Ibn Abbas: How can there be expiation of an oath in obedience of Satan. Ibn Abbas said: And those who zihar from their women, then he placed therein of the expiation what you have seen."
"15438","Ibn Jurayj informed me, he said: Ata informed me, that a man came to Ibn Abbas and said: I vowed I would surely slaughter myself, so Ibn Abbas said: You had in the Messenger of Allah a good example, then recited: And We ransomed him with a great sacrifice. Then he ordered him to slaughter a ram. He said: And I heard Ata, when asked: where does he slaughter the ram, say: At Makkah. I said: So he vowed he would surely slaughter his horse or his mule, he said: A camel I would order him, or a cow. I said: Ibn Abbas ordered a ram for the self, and you say for the animal: a camel. But he refused but that, twice."
"15439","Ma'mar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, he said: I think, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Whoever vows to slaughter himself or his child, let him slaughter a ram. Then he recited: You had in the Messenger of Allah a good example. From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad saying: A woman asked Ibn Abbas, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Jurayj from Yahya ibn Sa'id."
"15440","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ibn Tawus: A slave gave me glad tidings of something so I freed him, while he is not mine, and his family will sell him to me if I wish, how did your father say. He said: He used to say: None frees but one who owns, and he did not deem his freeing anything."
"15441","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, concerning a man who vowed he would surely slaughter himself, he said: Let him offer a hundred camels. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, I know it only from Ibn Abbas, the like of it."
"15442","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn Abbas, that a man asked him and said: I vowed to slaughter myself, he said: Do you find a hundred camels. He said: Yes. He said: Slaughter them. When the man turned away, Ibn Abbas said: Yet if I had ordered him a ram it would have sufficed him."
"15443","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, that Ikrimah informed him, that a man came to Ibn Abbas and said: I have committed a sin; if you order me I will slaughter myself this very hour, by Allah I will not inform you of it. Ibn Abbas said: Nay, perhaps I will inform you of its expiation. He said: What is it. So he ordered him a hundred she-camels."
"15444","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Musa narrating to Ata, that a man came to Ibn Umar and said: I vowed I would surely slaughter myself, he said: Fulfill what you vowed. He said: Shall I kill myself. He said: Then you will enter the Fire. He said: You have confused me. He said: You have confused yourself. Then he came to Ibn Abbas, who ordered him to slaughter a ram."
"15445","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ayyub ibn A'idh, he said: I asked al-Sha'bi about some matter, so Masruq said: A vow is two vows: what is for Allah, then fulfilling it and expiation; and what is for Satan, then no fulfilling it. He said: I said: Is there in obedience of Satan. He said: Perhaps you are of the analogizers. He said: I knew none more seeking of knowledge in any horizon than Masruq."
"15446","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Rishdin ibn Kurayb, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: A man and his mother came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he wanted jihad and his mother prevented him, so he said: Stay with your mother, for you have in reward with her like what you have in jihad."
"15447","He said: And another man came to him and said: I vowed to slaughter myself, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was occupied, and the man went and was found wanting to slaughter himself, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Praise be to Allah who placed in my Ummah one who fulfills the vow and fears a day whose evil is widespread. Have you wealth. He said: Yes. He said: Offer a hundred she-camels, and spread them over three years, for you will not find one to take them from you together."
"15448","Then a woman came to him and said: I am the messenger of women to you; by Allah, there is no woman among them, whether she knew or knew not, but she yearns for my coming out to you. Allah is Lord of women and men, and their God, and you are the Messenger of Allah to men and women. Allah prescribed jihad upon men; if they gain they are rewarded, and if martyred they are alive with their Lord, provided for. What equals that for women. He said: Their obedience to their husbands, and knowing their rights, and few of you do it."
"15449","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Amr ibn Shu'ayb saying: A man came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, upon whom was a vow to slaughter at Buwanah, he said: And Buwanah is a water at a fortress of Najd, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: If it was not an idol, or a festival of the festivals of the Jahiliyyah, then slaughter upon it. They claimed this man was Kurz ibn Sufyan."
"15450","From Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya, and Ibn Jurayj, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, freedman of al-Mahri, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: O Allah, I seek refuge in You that my grave be taken as an idol, and my pulpit as a festival."
"15451","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, he said: A'ishah and Ibn Abbas informed me that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when death descended upon him, began casting a striped garment of his upon his face; when distressed he uncovered it from his face, saying: The curse of Allah upon the Jews and Christians, they took the graves of their prophets as mosques. He said: A'ishah says: Warning against the like of what they did."
"15452","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Al-Hasan ibn Muslim said: When a woman vows without her husband's order, if he wishes he prevents her; if he prevents her, then let her give charity or do good in her vow, and he disliked her husband preventing her when she vowed."
"15453","Ma'mar informed us, from Haram ibn Uthman al-Ansari, from Abd Allah and Muhammad, sons of Jabir, from their father Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: No oath for a child with a parent, no oath for a wife with her husband's oath, no oath for a slave with his master's oath, no oath in severing ties, no vow in disobedience, no divorce before marriage, no freeing before ownership, no silence a day till night, no continuous fasting, no orphanhood after puberty, no suckling after weaning, no desert-dwelling after migration, and no migration after the Conquest."
"15454","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq informed me, that al-Walid ibn Malik ibn Abd al-Qays informed him, that Muhammad ibn Qays, freedman of Sahl ibn Hunayf, informed him, that Sahl ibn Hunayf informed him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: You are my messenger to the people of Makkah, say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent me reciting peace upon you, and ordering you with three: do not swear by other than Allah, when you relieve yourselves do not face the qiblah nor turn your backs to it, and do not cleanse with a bone nor with dung."
"15455","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Do not swear except by Allah; whoever swears by Allah, let him be truthful."
"15456","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, heard me swearing by my father, so he said: Allah forbids you to swear by your fathers. Umar said: By Allah, I never swore thereafter, deliberately or narrating."
"15457","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, caught up with me while I was among riders, swearing and saying: by my father, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Allah forbids you to swear by your fathers; whoever swears, let him swear by Allah, or be silent."
"15458","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq informed me, that Nafi' informed him, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, heard me swearing by my father, so he said: O Umar, do not swear by your father, swear by Allah, and do not swear by other than Allah. He said: So I never swore thereafter except by Allah."
"15459","He said: And he saw me urinating standing, so he said: O Umar, do not urinate standing. So I never urinated thereafter standing."
"15460","From Isra'il, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, he said: I was among riders traveling in a campaign with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, so I swore and said: No, by my father, so a man behind me rebuked me and said: Do not swear by your fathers. He said: So I turned, and there I was with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"15461","Al-Thawri informed us, from his father, and al-A'mash, and Mansur, from Sa'd ibn Ubaydah, from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar used to swear: by my father, so the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade him and said: Whoever swears by anything besides Allah has associated, or he said: Lo, he is a polytheist."
"15462","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah informing, that he heard Ibn al-Zubayr informing, that when Umar was at al-Mikhmas of Usfan, the people raced, and Umar outran them, so Ibn al-Zubayr said: So I seized the moment and outran him, and said: I outran him, by the Ka'bah. Then he seized the moment and outran me, and said: I outran him, by Allah. Then I seized the moment and outran him, and said: I outran him, by the Ka'bah. Then he seized the third time and outran me, and said: I outran him, by Allah. Then he made his camel kneel and said: See your swearing by the Ka'bah; by Allah, if I knew you thought about it before you swore I would have punished you. Swear by Allah, then sin or be dutiful."
"15463","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abu al-Jahhaf, from a man, from al-Sha'bi, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by a man saying: by my father, so he said: A people were punished, among them better than your father, so we are free of you until you return."
"15464","From al-Thawri, from Abu Salamah, from Wabarah, he said: Abd Allah said, I know not whether Ibn Mas'ud or Ibn Umar: That I swear by Allah falsely is more beloved to me than that I swear by other than Him truthfully."
"15465","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Tamimah al-Hujaymi, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by a man saying to his wife: O little sister, so he rebuked him; and passed by a man saying: by trust, and said: You said: by trust. You said: by trust."
"15466","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever swears and says in his oath: by al-Lat, let him say: There is no god but Allah; and whoever says to his companion: come, I will gamble with you, let him give something in charity."
"15467","Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, he said: It is disliked that a man swear by freeing or divorce, and that he swear except by Allah; and it was disliked to swear by the mushaf."
"15468","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Ata saying: Khalid ibn al-As and Shaybah ibn Uthman used to say when they swore: by my father, so Abu Hurayrah forbade them that, to swear by their fathers. He said: So Shaybah changed and said: by my life; that because a man asked Ata about la'amri and about laha Allahu idhan, is there harm in them, so he said: No. Then he narrated this hadith from Abu Hurayrah, and I say: So long as it was not swearing by other than Allah, there is no harm; la'amri is not an oath."
"15469","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard a man ask Ata and say: I swore by the House, or said: by the Book of Allah, he said: They are not a lord to you, it is not an oath."
"15470","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: Whoever says: I bear witness, I swear, then it is nothing; and when he says: I swore, while he did not swear, then it is a lie."
"15471","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, I know it only as raised, he said: Do not swear by idols, nor by your fathers, nor by trust."
"15472","Ma'mar informed us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he disliked: la'amruka, and saw no harm in: la'amri."
"15473","Ma'mar said: And al-Hasan used to say: No harm in: aymu Allah, and say: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: By Him in whose hand is my soul."
"15474","Ma'mar informed us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he disliked: waymu Allah wherever it be, and saw no harm in saying: waymu Allah."
"15475","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, that Ibn Umar disliked a man saying: By Allah wherever it be."
"15476","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abd Rabbihi, from Mujahid, that he disliked a man saying: it is alleged."
"15477","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Zahdam al-Jarmi, that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: By Allah."
"15478","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from Ibn Umar, he said: By Allah, in the hadith of Ghaylan ibn Salamah."
"15479","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: When he says: I swore, while he did not swear, then it is an oath."
"15480","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab and Mu'adh ibn Afra disputed, so they appointed Ubayy ibn Ka'b as arbiter, and came to him. Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The arbiter is visited at his house. So he judged an oath upon Umar, and he swore, then Mu'adh gave it to him."
"15481","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he disliked one saying: No, praise be to Allah."
"15482","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: Whoever disbelieves in a letter of the Qur'an has disbelieved in all of it; and whoever swears by the Qur'an, upon him for every verse of it is an oath."
"15483","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abd Allah ibn Murrah, from Abu Kanaf, that Ibn Mas'ud passed by a man saying: by Surah al-Baqarah, so he said: Do you see him expiating. Yet upon him for every verse of it is an oath."
"15484","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever swears by a surah of the Qur'an, upon him for every verse is a binding oath; whoever wishes, let him fulfill it, and whoever wishes, let him violate it."
"15485","From Ma'mar, he said: One who heard al-Hasan informed me, he saying: Whoever swears by a surah of the Qur'an, upon him for every verse of it is a binding oath."
"15486","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Mas'ud, that he heard a man saying: by Surah al-Baqarah, swearing by it, so he said: Yet upon him for every letter of it is an oath."
"15487","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata informed me, that he came to A'ishah, Mother of the Believers, with Ubayd ibn Umayr, while she was staying in devotion in the interior of Thabir in the region of Mina, so Ubayd said: O dear, what is the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: Allah will not take you to task for idle talk in your oaths. She said: It is a man saying: No, by Allah, and Yes, by Allah."
"15488","Ubayd said: O dear, when is migration. She said: No migration after the Conquest; migration was only before the Conquest, when a man would migrate with his religion to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. As for when the Conquest came, wherever a man wishes he worships Allah, he is not lost. Ibn Jurayj said: I said to Ata: What about: but He will take you to task for what you contracted of oaths. He said: By Allah, besides whom there is no god. He said: I said to him: For something he intends and understands, my saying: By Allah I will not do it, while I did not contract, except that by Allah I said: I will not do it, he said: And that too is of what your hearts earned, and recited: but He will take you to task for what your hearts earned."
"15489","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah, she said: They are people disputing in a matter, this saying: No, by Allah, and Yes, by Allah, and Nay, by Allah, disputing in the matter, their hearts not binding upon it."
"15490","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: It is a man swearing about a thing, seeing it is so while it is not so. But He will take you to task for what you contracted of oaths, he said: That you swear about a thing while you know it."
"15491","From Hushaym ibn Bashir, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: It is a man swearing upon the unlawful, so Allah does not take him to task for leaving it."
"15492","From Hushaym, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: It is a man swearing about a thing then forgetting. Hushaym said: And Mansur informed me, from al-Hasan, the like of the saying of Ibrahim."
"15493","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: It is unintentional mistake, like a man's saying: By Allah this is so and so, while he deems himself truthful but it is not so. And Qatadah said it."
"15494","From Umar ibn Dharr, he said: I heard al-Sha'bi saying: Piety and sin is what he swore upon his knowledge, while he deems it so; there is no sin in it, nor expiation upon him."
"15495","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab informed me, that Ibn Shihab informed him, that Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab informed him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Oaths promote the commodity and efface wealth."
"15496","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, he said: Ibn Mas'ud passed by a man selling his commodity, so he struck him with the whip. When he passed, the man asked about him, and it was said to him: He is Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud. He said to him: Why did you strike me. He said: Because you swear, and swearing promotes sale and effaces blessing."
"15497","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'qub, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: A false oath promotes the commodity and effaces earning."
"15498","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Qays, I think he said Ibn Gharazah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Trade is attended by idle talk and oaths, so mix it with something of charity, or of charity."
"15499","From Abd Allah ibn Kathir, from Shu'bah ibn al-Hajjaj, he said: Habib ibn Abi Thabit narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Wa'il narrating, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came out to us while we were selling in the market, we being called brokers, and said: O company of merchants, this market of yours is mixed with idle talk and oaths, so mix it with something of charity, or of charity."
"15500","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, he said: I heard Mujahid saying: Iblis comes with his caravan, places it in the market, and the Throne does not cease shaking from what Allah knows, and Allah bears witness to what He did not witness."
"15501","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Ibn Abbas said: Let none of you say: Allah knows it, while He does not know it, so Allah knows what He did not know, and that is grave with Allah."
"15502","Ma'mar informed us, from Ibn Abi Ya'la, he said: I heard Sa'id ibn Jubayr saying: When a servant says of a thing that was not, Allah knows that; Allah, mighty and majestic, says: My servant was unable to inform other than Me."
"15503","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa asked Ata and said: It is disapproved with us, and he says: It is deceit that a man bargains a man for his commodity, then the one bargained swears he will not sell it for that, while concealing in himself selling for that, and expiating for his oath."
"15504","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: When he says: I adjure you by Allah, then he should not make him break it; if he does, the one who swore expiates."
"15505","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata asked about a man who adjured a man then made him break it, upon which of them is the expiation. He said: Upon the breaker. Then I asked him after, and he said: The like of that."
"15506","Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, he said: The expiation is upon the one who broke, and the sin upon the one who made him break; and it is not an oath until he says: I adjure you by Allah. As for if he says: I adjured, then it is nothing."
"15507","Ma'mar informed us, he said: One who heard Ikrimah narrating from Abu Hurayrah informed me, that he said: Whoever adjures a man while deeming he will fulfill him, but he does not fulfill him, then his sin is upon the one who did not fulfill him."
"15508","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed that a freedwoman of A'ishah, Mother of the Believers, adjured her concerning a piece of dried meat to eat it, so A'ishah broke her, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, placed the expiation of the oath upon A'ishah."
"15509","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Thabit, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever swears by a religion other than Islam falsely, then he is as he said."
"15510","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: When he says: I swore, or I swore by Allah, then it is an oath; or says: I bear witness, or I bear witness by Allah, then it is an oath; or says: upon me is Allah's covenant and pact, then it is an oath; or says: upon me is a vow, or upon me to Allah is a vow, then it is an oath; or: a Jew, or a Christian, or a Magian, then it is an oath; or: free of Islam, then it is an oath; or says: upon me is a bond, or upon me is Allah's bond, then it is an oath."
"15511","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Mansur, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, concerning a man who says: he is a Jew, or a Christian, or a Magian, or free of Islam, or upon him is Allah's curse, or upon him is a vow, he said: An emphasized oath."
"15512","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Whoever says: I am a disbeliever, or I am a Jew, or a Christian, or a Magian, or may Allah disgrace me, or the like of that, then it is an oath he expiates."
"15513","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, concerning a man who says: may Allah disgrace me, may Allah cut my hand, may Allah crucify me, may Allah do to me, supplicating against himself, he said: It is nothing. Jabir said: And al-Hakam said: Most beloved to me is that he expiate."
"15514","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard a man say to Ata: A man said: upon me is Allah's anger, or may Allah disgrace me, or I called upon Allah against myself with something, shall I expiate. He said: It is most beloved to me if you do. He said: If I do not. He said: Nothing is upon you, it is not an oath."
"15515","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata asked about a man's saying: upon me is Allah's covenant and pact, then he breaks it, is it an oath. He said: No, unless he intended an oath. Or he says: may Allah disgrace me, or says: upon me is Allah's curse, or says: I associate with Allah, or I disbelieve in Allah, or the like of that, he said: No, except what he swore by Allah, mighty and majestic."
"15516","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, about a man who says: 'The covenant of Allah and His compact are upon me' or 'The covenant of Allah is upon me' – he said: It is an oath for which he expiates."
"15517","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: that he used to regard the solemn swearing (qasam) as an oath."
"15518","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: The covenant is an oath."
"15519","From al-Thawri, from Firas, from al-Sha'bi, who said: The covenant is an oath."
"15520","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: What is the emphatic oath? He did not single out for me any oath apart from another as being the emphatic one. I said: You once told me: swearing by emancipation is among the emphatic oaths, in it is the freeing of a slave – is emancipation likewise? He said: Nothing has reached me about it, and I dislike saying anything about it; that you free a slave concerning it, if you did it, is dearer to me."
"15521","Ma'mar reported to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Qilabah, from Thabit ibn al-Dahhak, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no vow concerning what you do not own, and cursing a believer is like killing him, and whoever kills himself with something in this world will be punished with it on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever swears by a religion other than Islam falsely is as he said, and whoever says to a believer: O disbeliever! – it is like killing him."
"15522","Ma'mar reported to us, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, about a man who says: 'I testify,' 'I swore,' 'I vowed' – they both said: It is nothing until he says: I swear by Allah, I swore by Allah."
"15523","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah, that Abu Bakr said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the dream when he had interpreted it: I adjure you, by my father, you will tell me what I got wrong – so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not swear – and he did not order expiation."
"15524","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata reported to me, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from Aishah, Mother of the Believers, that she asked her – or he heard her asking – about an oath-taker who swore and said: My wealth is a tribute in the locks of the Ka'bah, or in the path of Allah – so she said: It is an oath upon him. And Hatim, Ata's son-in-law, reported to me that he was Ata's messenger to Safiyyah about that."
"15525","From al-Thawri, from Mansur ibn Safiyyah, from his mother Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from Aishah, that she was asked about a man who placed all his wealth in the locks of the Ka'bah, concerning something between him and an aunt of his – Aishah said: What expiates an oath expiates it. Ma'mar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Aishah the like of it. From Ma'mar, who said: One who heard al-Hasan and Ikrimah reported to me that they both said the like of Aishah's saying."
"15526","Ibn Jurayj reported to us, who said: Ibn Tawus reported to me, from his father, that he used to say: Swearing by emancipation, and 'everything of mine is in the path of Allah,' and 'my wealth is a sacrificial offering,' and the like of this, is an oath among oaths, its expiation is the expiation of an oath."
"15527","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata was asked about a man who said: A thousand sacrificial camels are upon me – he said: It is an oath. And about a man who said: A thousand pilgrimages are upon me – he said: It is an oath. And about a man who said: My wealth is an offering – he said: It is an oath. And about a man who said: My wealth is for the poor – he said: It is an oath. From al-Thawri, who said: One who heard al-Hasan reported to me that he said about it the like of Ata's saying. He said: And al-Sha'bi and Ibrahim used to bind every man to what he imposed upon himself."
"15528","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, who said: A man came to Ibn Umar and said: I have placed my wealth in the path of Allah – Ibn Umar said: Then it is in the path of Allah."
"15529","Al-Zuhri said: I have not heard about this sort of thing any course except what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Lubabah: A third suffices you – and to Ka'b ibn Malik: Keep some of your wealth for yourself, for it is better for you."
"15530","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: A man said: My camels are a vow, or an offering – he said: Perhaps one camel will suffice him, if his camels are many."
"15531","From Umar ibn Dharr, who said: I heard a man asking Ata ibn Abi Rabah about a man who made his camels an offering – so he said: Let him look for a fat slaughter-camel and offer it, then keep the rest of his camels."
"15532","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Khalid reported to me, that Abdullah ibn Sufyan reported to him, that a man from the people of Rahit said to a slave-boy of his: Bring out the crowbar or the pickaxe – so the boy said: It is in the house, I will bring it out – then his master entered seeking it and did not find it, so he went out to the boy and said: I do not find it – he said: It is in the house – he said: Go in, and if you find it you are free – so the boy went in and found it and brought it out. Uthman said: Ibn Sufyan reported to me that he wrote about that to Abd al-Malik, and that he wrote to him: That is but void; it is only an oath."
"15533","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Uthman ibn Abi Hadir, who said: A woman from the people of Dhu Asbah swore and said: My wealth is in the path of Allah, and my slave-girl is free, if such-and-such does not happen – about something her husband disliked – then her husband swore he would not do it – so Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas were asked about that, and they both said: As for the slave-girl, she is freed; and as for her saying 'my wealth is in the path of Allah,' she gives the zakat of her wealth in charity."
"15534","Ma'mar reported to us, from Qatadah, from Jabir ibn Zayd, who was asked about a man who made his wealth an offering in the path of Allah – so he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, did not intend to strip anyone of his wealth; if it is much, let him offer a fifth of it, and if it is middling, a seventh, and if it is little, a tenth. Qatadah said: Much is two thousand, middling is a thousand, and little is five hundred."
"15535","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Bakr ibn Abdullah al-Muzani, who said: Abu Rafi' reported to me, who said: My mistress Layla bint al-Ajma said to me: Every slave she owns is free, and all her wealth is an offering, and she is a Jewess and a Christian, if you do not divorce your wife, or separate between you and your wife. He said: So I went to Zaynab bint Umm Salamah – and when a woman was mentioned for understanding, Zaynab was mentioned – he said: So she came with me to her and said: Is Harut and Marut in the house? She said: O Zaynab! May Allah make me your ransom, she said: Every slave she owns is free, and she is a Jewess and a Christian. She said: A Jewess and a Christian? Leave the man with his wife. He said: It was as if she did not accept that. He said: So I went to Hafsah, and she sent with me to her, and she said: O Mother of the Believers! May Allah make me your ransom, she said: Every slave she owns is free, and all her wealth is an offering, and she is a Jewess and a Christian. Hafsah said: A Jewess and a Christian? Leave the man with his wife. It was as if she refused. So I went to Abdullah ibn Umar, and he went with me to her, and when he greeted she recognized his voice and said: May my father be your ransom, and your father be the ransom of my forefathers! He said: Are you made of stones, or of iron, or of what thing are you? Zaynab gave you a ruling, and the Mother of the Believers gave you a ruling, and you did not accept from them both. She said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, may Allah make me your ransom, she said: Every slave she owns is free, and all her wealth is an offering, and she is a Jewess and a Christian. He said: A Jewess and a Christian? Expiate for your oath, and leave the man with his wife. From Ma'mar, from Aban, from Bakr ibn Abdullah al-Muzani, from Abu Rafi', from Ibn Umar the like of it, except that he did not mention: every slave she owns is free."
"15536","From al-Thawri, about a man who says to a man: I will offer you as a sacrifice, then breaks it – he said: al-Mughirah reported to me, from Ibrahim, and Firas, from al-Sha'bi, they both said: He performs pilgrimage with him."
"15537","From Ibrahim ibn Umar, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from Ibrahim, who said: He performs pilgrimage with him, and offers a slaughter-camel."
"15538","Abd al-Karim said: And Ata said, from Ibn Abbas: He offers a ram, and does not perform pilgrimage with him."
"15539","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, who said: He offers a sheep."
"15540","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from Ali, who said: He offers a sacrificial camel."
"15541","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: He offers a sacrificial camel. And al-Hasan said: He expiates his oath."
"15542","Ma'mar reported to me, who said: One who asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr reported to me, about a brother of his who said: I will offer this slave-girl of mine – he said: He offers her price in sacrificial camels. Ma'mar said: And Qatadah used to say about the likes of this: a sacrificial camel. Ma'mar said: And al-Hasan used to say: He expiates for his oath."
"15543","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: When he offers something as a gift, let him carry it through."
"15544","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Ismail, from a man, who said: So I met that man myself, and he said: I heard al-Sha'bi being asked about a woman who borrowed a cooking pot and said: If it is with me, then I will offer it as a gift – and she did not think it was with her, though it was with her – al-Sha'bi said: She offers its price."
"15545","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: Whoever says: My wealth is a tribute in the locks of the Ka'bah, or in the path of Allah – it is in the position of an oath, for which he expiates. He said: And one who heard al-Hasan and Ikrimah reported to me that they both say the like of that. Ma'mar said: And it is dearer to me, if he is well-off, that he free a slave."
"15546","Ma'mar reported to us, from Qatadah, about a man who said: The freeing of a hundred slaves is upon me – then he broke it – he said: He frees one slave. And Uthman al-Batti said: He frees a hundred slaves, as he said."
"15547","From al-Thawri, who said: Ibrahim and al-Sha'bi used to be strict about it; they bound every man to what he imposed upon himself – when he says: A hundred slaves are upon me, or a hundred pilgrimages, or a hundred sacrificial camels."
"15548","From Ma'mar, from Aban and Sulayman al-Taymi, from Bakr ibn Abdullah al-Muzani, from Abu Rafi', that he heard Ibn Umar – and a woman asked him and said: She swore and said: She is one day a Jewess and one day a Christian, and her wealth is in the path of Allah, and the likes of this – so Ibn Umar said: Expiate for your oath."
"15549","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: Whoever says: The freeing of a slave is upon me – then breaks it – he said: It is an oath. Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Hasan reported to me that he says the like of it. Abu Urwah said: And it is dearer to me, if he is well-off, that he free a slave."
"15550","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: It was narrated to me from Muhammad al-Ash'ari, who said: Tawus bought a slave-girl and left her with me for a year, then he passed by me and called for her to take her away, and said to me and to another with me: Ibn Yusuf – no beautiful slave-girl is mentioned to him but he sends for her – and I call you both to witness that I have freed her, off the tip of my tongue, I do not say it from myself to use it as a pretext, if Muhammad sends for her. And I heard Zam'ah saying: Muhammad al-Ash'ari reported to me, then he mentioned this hadith."
"15551","From Israel, from Abd al-A'la, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Whoever owes a slave from the children of Ismail – nothing suffices him except from us (i.e. Arabs)."
"15552","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, that a man said to Ibn Umar: I have imposed upon myself the freeing of a slave from the children of Ismail – he said: Then free al-Hasan ibn Ali."
"15553","Ibn Uyaynah said: And a man said to Umar: A slave from the children of Ismail is upon me – he said: Then free Ali ibn Abi Talib."
"15554","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Qatadah, from one who witnessed the company among whom was Umar, that Umar said: Whoever owes a freed slave from the children of Ismail – let him not free anyone from Himyar."
"15555","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from a man, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, who said: Oaths are four: two oaths that are expiated, and two oaths that are not expiated. When he says: By Allah, I did it – and he did not do it – it is a lie. And when he says: By Allah, I did not do it – though he did – it is a lie. And when he says: By Allah, I shall do it – and does not do it – it is an oath. Or he says: By Allah, I shall not do it – then does it – it is an oath."
"15556","From Ibrahim ibn Yahya, from Amr, from al-Hasan, who said: Oaths are four: two oaths that are expiated, and two oaths that are not expiated; in the latter two are seeking forgiveness and repentance. Then he mentioned the like of al-Nakha'i's saying."
"15557","Ma'mar reported to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, about a man who swears about a matter falsely, deliberately – saying: By Allah, I did it – though he did not do it; By Allah, I did not do it – though he did – there is no expiation in it; he says: It is graver than that. Ma'mar said: And it is dearer to me that he expiate."
"15558","Ma'mar said: And Qatadah said al-Hasan said: When he says: By Allah, I shall do it – and does not do it – he expiates; and when he says: By Allah, I shall not do it – then does it – he expiates."
"15559","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ismail ibn Umayyah reported to me, from a trustworthy man of the people of Madinah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your oath is upon what your companion believes you about."
"15560","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ismail ibn Kathir reported to me, from Aishah, who said: The oath is upon what you are believed about."
"15561","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, who said: Tawus used to say about a man who swears by Allah to a man over his right, while the swearer intends what the one sworn to does not suppose – he said: That is upon what the one sworn to supposed, as if he swore and made an exception in himself, or equivocated in the oath."
"15562","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: When he swears while wronged, the intention is his intention; and when he swears while a wrongdoer, the intention is the intention of the one who made him swear."
"15563","Ibn Jurayj reported to us, who said: A man came to Ata and said: I swore an oath, I do not know what it is – divorce or something else? He said: That is only Satan; expiate for your oath, and act."
"15564","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: that a man bargained with Ibn Umar over a garment, and the man swore he would not sell it to him, then it occurred to him to sell it – so Ibn Umar disliked buying it because of his oath."
"15565","Ma'mar and al-Thawri reported to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Wahb, who said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal passed by a man selling sheep and bargained with him for them, and the man swore he would not sell them; then he passed by him after that when they had become unsalable, and he offered them to him – so Mu'adh said to him: You had sworn, and he disliked buying them."
"15566","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: There is no harm in buying them."
"15567","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not compel people to their oaths, so that they swear about what they do not know."
"15568","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I asked Ata and said to him: I swore about a matter, and something else is better than it – shall I leave it and expiate for my oath? He said: Yes."
"15569","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abdullah ibn Kathir reported to me that he heard a man asking Abu al-Sha'tha, saying: I swore an oath, and something else is better than it – so Abu al-Sha'tha said: Expiate for your oath and do what is better."
"15570","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan and Muhammad ibn Sirin, who both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever swears an oath and sees something else better than it, let him do what is better and expiate for his oath."
"15571","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not swear except by Allah; whoever swears by Allah, let him be truthful; and whoever swears an oath and sees something else better than it, let him do what is better and expiate for his oath."
"15572","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Zahdam al-Jarmi, who said: I was with Abu Musa al-Ash'ari when food containing chicken was brought to him, and a man of Banu Abs stood up and withdrew – so Abu Musa said to him: Come near! I have seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eating them. He said: I saw them eating something I found filthy, so I swore I would not eat them. He said: Come near, until I tell you about your oath too! I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a group of my people and said: O Messenger of Allah, mount us – so he swore he would not mount us; then booty of camels came to him, and he ordered five camels for us. We said: We have tricked the oath of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); by Allah, if we go with them on this basis we shall not prosper. So we returned to him and said: O Prophet of Allah, you swore you would not mount us, then you mounted us – so he said: Allah, Blessed and Exalted, is the One who mounted you; and I never swear about a matter and see what is better than it except I do what is better and release myself."
"15573","From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you persists in an oath against his family, he is sinful; the expiation Allah ordered is due from him. From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"15574","From Ibn Jurayj and Ma'mar, who both said: Hisham ibn Urwah reported to us, from Urwah, from Aishah, that she reported to him that Abu Bakr never broke an oath he swore, until Allah sent down the expiation of oaths – then he said: By Allah, I shall not leave an oath I swore, while I see something else better than it, except I accept Allah's concession and do what is better."
"15575","From Ibn Jurayj and Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to say: If a man swears to disobey Allah, let him expiate and leave it, so that he has the reward of what he left and the reward of what he expiated for his oath."
"15576","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Sulayman al-Ahwal, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Whoever swears over his slave that he will beat him – the expiation of his oath is that he not beat him, and with the expiation it is a good deed."
"15577","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: I said to him: A man swore he would beat his slave – he said: Breaking it is dearer to me than his beating him."
"15578","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, who said: We were with Ibn Mas'ud when an udder was brought, and a man withdrew – so Abdullah said: Come near. He said: I have forbidden the udder to myself. He recited: O you who believe, do not forbid the good things Allah made lawful for you – eat, and expiate."
"15579","From Israel, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: A man vowed he would not eat with the orphan sons of a brother of his, and Umar was told of it and said: Go and eat with them – so he did."
"15580","Al-Thawri reported to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, that a man with smallpox went out to the desert seeking medicine, and met a man who described to him the arak tree, cooking it – or he said: arak water with camel urine – and took from him a pledge that he would not tell anyone; so he did it and recovered; then when people saw him they asked him, but he refused to tell them; so they began bringing the sick to him and laying them at his door – then he asked Ibn Mas'ud, who said: You have met a man in whose heart there is no mercy for anyone; describe it to people."
"15581","From Israel ibn Yunus, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay', from Mujahid, who said: A man stayed as guest with a man of the Ansar, and he came in the evening and said: Have you had supper? They said: No, we waited for you. He said: You waited for me until this hour? By Allah, I shall not taste it. The wife said: By Allah, I shall not taste it if you do not taste it. The guest said: By Allah, I shall not eat if you do not eat. When the man saw that, he said: I shall not combine keeping myself, my guest, and my wife from it – so he put his hand and ate. In the morning he went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him the story – so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What did you do? He said: I ate, O Prophet of Allah! The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You obeyed Allah and disobeyed Satan."
"15582","From Israel, from Abd al-Aziz, from Tamim ibn Tarafah, who said: I heard Adi ibn Abi Hatim come to a dwelling and alight there, and a Bedouin came asking him – so he said: I have nothing with me to give you, but I have a coat of mail at Kufah, it is yours – so the Bedouin scorned it, and he swore he would not give it to him. Then he said: I came only asking you about a servant, that you help me in it – so he said: I have assigned my coat of mail to you; by Allah, it is dearer to me than three slaves – so the Bedouin desired it and said: I accept your kindness. Adi said: Were it not that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever swears an oath and sees something else better than it, let him follow what is better – I would not have given you."
"15583","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: It reached me that some Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disputed one day about some matter of the fifth while dividing it, and when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw what they reached, he swore they would not divide it; then when it was lifted from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he ordered its division – so Umar said: O Messenger of Allah! Had you not sworn it would not be divided? By Allah, our paying it from our wealth is dearer to me than your sinning in it. He said: I did not sin in it; whoever swears an oath while something else is better than it, let him do what is better and expiate for his oath."
"15584","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, about His saying: And do not make Allah an excuse in your oaths – he said: It is a man who swears over a matter that is not right, then excuses himself by his oath; and he says: Allah says: to do good and fear – he says: It is better than persisting in what is not right; so if you swore, expiate for your oath and do what is better."
"15585","From al-Thawri, from a man, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: Expiation becomes obligatory in the oath upon one who has three dirhams."
"15586","From al-Thawri, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Farqad, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, who said: It is not obligatory upon him until he has twenty dirhams."
"15587","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: When he does not find what to feed with in the expiation of the oath, he fasts three days."
"15588","From al-Thawri, from Ya'la ibn Ata, who said: One who heard Abu Hurayrah reported to me that he says: Fasting in the expiation is only for one who does not find."
"15589","Ma'mar reported to us, from Qatadah, who said: When he has only a small thing, let him who breaks his oath fast."
"15590","Ma'mar reported to us, who said: Al-Zuhri was asked about a man upon whom an oath falls, who wants to ransom his oath – he said: It was done; Ubayd al-Siham ransomed his oath for ten thousand during Marwan's governorship, while the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madinah were many."
"15591","From Ismail, from Sharik, from Abdullah, who said: al-Aswad ibn Qays narrated to us, from a man of his people, who said: Hudhayfah recognized a camel of his with a man, and disputed with him, and the camel was judged to Hudhayfah, while the oath was judged against him – so Hudhayfah said: Ransom your oath for ten dirhams – but the man refused; so Hudhayfah said to him: For twenty? He refused. He said: For thirty? He refused. He said: For forty? But the man refused – so Hudhayfah said: Do you think I shall not swear over my property?! – then Hudhayfah swore over it."
"15592","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, who said: Ibn Umar would sometimes say to one of his sons: I have counted against you in this sitting eleven oaths, and he would not order him to expiate – meaning one expiation suffices him."
"15593","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn Suqah, who said: A man sat with Ibn Umar and heard him swearing much, and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman! Each time you swear, do you expiate for your oath? He said: By Allah, I did not swear – so Ibn Umar said: This too!"
"15594","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', who said: Ibn Umar, when he emphasized oaths and followed them up in a sitting, would free a slave. Ma'mar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar the like of it."
"15595","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: It was narrated to me that Ibn Umar said to a slave-boy of his – while Mujahid was listening, and he used to send that slave-boy of his to Syria: You tarry with your wife, a slave-girl of Abdullah's – divorce her. The boy said: No. Ibn Umar said: By Allah, you shall divorce her. The boy said: By Allah, I shall not do it – until Ibn Umar swore three times that he shall divorce her, and the slave swore he would not do it – so Abdullah said: The slave has beaten me. Mujahid said: So I said to Ibn Umar: How many expiations for them? He said: One expiation."
"15596","From al-Thawri, from Aban ibn Uthman, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, that he said: When I swear repeatedly, it is one expiation."
"15597","From al-Thawri, from Muhill, from Ibrahim, who said: When he repeats oaths, it is one oath. And Sufyan said: And we say, when he keeps repeating oaths intending one oath, it is one oath; and when he intends emphasis, every oath he repeated is an oath."
"15598","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to me that a man asked Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, saying: O Abu Abdullah! A slave-girl of mine exposed herself to me, so I swore I would not touch her; then she exposed herself to me, so I swore I would not touch her; then she exposed herself to me, so I swore I would not touch her – shall I expiate one expiation, or separate expiations? He said: It is one expiation."
"15599","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata said – a man said: By Allah, I shall not do such-and-such, about two different matters covered by the oath together – he said: One expiation. I said to him: By Allah, I shall not do such, by Allah, I shall not do such – about two different matters, in one utterance, but he specified each one with an oath – he said: Two expiations. He said: If he swears about one matter to different groups of people, or swears over it successive oaths, oaths with different oaths – their expiations are separate; he expiates them all if he breaks. He said: If he swears about one matter by Allah, in that is one expiation, so long as he has not expiated. All this is from Ata."
"15600","He said: Ma'mar reported to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, who said: They say: Whoever swears repeated oaths in one sitting, it is one expiation; and when in different sittings, they are different expiations."
"15601","Ma'mar reported to us, from Qatadah, who said: When he swears in one sitting, it is one expiation; and when in different sittings, they are different expiations."
"15602","Ma'mar reported to us, from al-Zuhri, who said: When he swears in different sittings, it is one expiation. Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Hasan and Ikrimah reported to me that they both say the like of al-Zuhri's saying, so long as he has not expiated."
"15603","Ma'mar reported to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Zayd ibn Thabit about the expiation of the oath, who said: Two mudds of wheat for each poor person. Ma'mar said: And I heard al-Zuhri narrating from Zayd ibn Thabit and Ibn Umar the like of it."
"15604","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: Two mudds of wheat for each poor person; and whoever does not find, then fasting three days."
"15605","Hisham ibn Hassan reported to us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: A mudd for each poor person."
"15606","From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: A mudd of wheat, a quarter of it with its condiment."
"15607","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: A mudd for each poor person, expiating for his oath by feeding ten poor persons, for each person a mudd of wheat."
"15608","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: A mudd for each poor person."
"15609","Al-Thawri reported to us, from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from Yasar ibn Numayr, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said to me: I swear I shall not give men, then it occurs to me and I give them; so when you see me do that, feed on my behalf ten poor persons, each poor person a sa' of barley, or a sa' of dates, or half a sa' of wheat. From Ma'mar, from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from Umar the like of it."
"15610","From Waki', from Ibn Abi Layla, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abdullah ibn Salamah, from Ali, who said: A sa' of barley, or half a sa' of wheat."
"15611","Hisham ibn Hassan reported to us, from al-Hasan, who said: A makkuk of wheat, or a makkuk of dates for each poor person, and he feeds each people by their mudd. Al-Hasan said: And if he wishes, he gathers them and feeds them a meal – bread and meat; and if he does not find, then bread and ghee and milk; and if he does not find, then bread and vinegar and oil; and if he does not find, he fasts three days."
"15612","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, who said: A makkuk of wheat, and a makkuk of dates; and if he wishes, he gathers the poor and feeds them lunch or supper."
"15613","Ma'mar reported to us, who said: Qatadah reported to me that he heard al-Hasan saying: A makkuk of wheat, and a makkuk of dates. Ma'mar said: And I heard Qatadah saying the like of al-Hasan's saying."
"15614","Ma'mar said: And I heard Qatadah ordering, in a year prices rose, half a makkuk of wheat and half a makkuk of dates; then he turned to his companions and said: I do not see any of you spending today more than this."
"15615","Al-Thawri reported to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, who said: I said to Sa'id ibn Jubayr about feeding food: Shall I gather them in my house and feed them? He said: No; two mudds for each poor person – a mudd for his food and a mudd for his condiment."
"15616","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: Two mudds for each poor person."
"15617","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Tawus reported to me, from his father, that he used to say: Feeding a day is not one meal, but a day of the average of what he feeds his family, for each poor person."
"15618","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: As you feed the single one of your family."
"15619","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata said: From the average of what he feeds his family, one day – ten mudds. He is the one saying: Or clothing them – he said: It reached us that it is a garment each. I said: It reached us that people say it suffices him to feed them one meal – but whatever he says, he did not trace it to its end, to his saying: they feed a day."
"15620","Ibn Jurayj reported to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar that he used to expiate for his oath by feeding ten poor persons, for each poor person a mudd of wheat. He said: And as for the oath he used to emphasize – if he found what to free, he freed. And Musa ibn Uqbah mentioned it."
"15621","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: You feed with the mudd with which you sustain your family."
"15622","Al-Thawri reported to us, from Abdullah ibn Hanash, who said: I asked al-Aswad ibn Yazid about His saying: From the average of what you feed your families – he said: Bread and dates."
"15623","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Nothing suffices him except feeding ten. He said: And one of our companions said, from al-Hasan or another: If he returns the food to one poor person, it suffices him."
"15624","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, who was asked about feeding the Jew and the Christian in the expiation – he said: It suffices him. And al-Hakam said: It does not suffice him. And Ibrahim said: I hope it suffices him when he does not find Muslims; and he gives the mukatab and the relative whom he is not compelled to maintain."
"15625","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, who said: Clothing is two garments each."
"15626","From Ja'far, who said: I heard Yazid al-Raqashi, from al-Hasan, that he said: A waist-wrap and a cloak – Zahrani Mu'aqqadah. He said: Clothes brought from Bahrain."
"15627","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Two garments each. And likewise al-Ash'ari Abu Musa clothed two garments each of Bahrain Mu'aqqadah. Ma'mar said: And Qatadah said: Two garments each, and likewise al-Ash'ari."
"15628","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Ibn Sirin, that Abu Musa al-Ash'ari clothed in the expiation of the oath two garments of Bahrain Mu'aqqadah."
"15629","From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said: Clothing is a turban with which he wraps his head, and a cloak in which he wraps himself."
"15630","Ma'mar reported to us, from al-Zuhri, who said: A waist-wrap and upward for each poor person."
"15631","Al-Thawri reported to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim about the clothing of the expiation, who said: One enveloping garment for each poor person."
"15632","Al-Thawri reported to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: Clothing – its least is a garment, and its most is what he wishes."
"15633","From al-Thawri, who said: Our saying about clothing is: if he clothes some of them and feeds some of them, it suffices him, when the clothing is worth food."
"15634","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: A garment for each poor person."
"15635","From Hisham ibn Muhammad, that Abu Musa al-Ash'ari swore an oath, and it occurred to him to expiate – so he clothed two garments each of Bahrain Mu'aqqadah. He said: And he swore another time, and kneaded dough for them and fed them."
"15636","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard Ata saying: It reached us in Ibn Mas'ud's reading: So whoever does not find, then fasting three consecutive days. He said: And likewise we read it."
"15637","Ma'mar reported to us, from Abu Ishaq and al-A'mash, who both said in Ibn Mas'ud's letters: Then fasting three consecutive days. Abu Ishaq said: And likewise we read it."
"15638","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, who said: A man came to Tawus and asked him about fasting three days in the expiation of the oath – he said: Fast as you wish. Mujahid said to him: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, it is in Ibn Mas'ud's reading: consecutive. He said: Then tell the man."
"15639","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, who said: Every fast in the Qur'an is consecutive, except the make-up of Ramadan."
"15640","From al-Thawri, from al-Hakam, who said: When he fasts two days in the expiation of the oath, then finds the expiation, he feeds."
"15641","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: He used to swear, then want to do what he swore he would not do – so he would expiate once before doing it, then do it after; and he would do it once before expiating, then expiate after doing it. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar the like of it; he said: Then I heard it from Ubaydullah."
"15642","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard Yazid ibn Ibrahim – or one who heard him narrating reported to me – from Ibn Sirin, who said: Salman used to expiate before breaking."
"15643","From al-Aslami, from a man he named, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas: that he used not to expiate until he broke."
"15644","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: Whoever swears and says: By Allah, God willing – there is no expiation upon him. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar the like of it; then he heard it from Ubaydullah."
"15645","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', who said: Ibn Umar used to swear, saying: By Allah, I shall not do such-and-such, God willing – then do it, then not expiate. Ma'mar reported to us, from al-Zuhri, the like of Ibn Umar's saying; Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Hasan reported to me that he says it."
"15646","From al-Thawri and Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, and from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Mas'ud, who both said: Whoever swears and says: God willing – he has not broken."
"15647","From Ibn Mujahid, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Whoever makes an exception – there is no breaking upon him and no expiation."
"15648","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, who said: Abu Dharr said: There is no man who says in the morning: O Allah, whatever word I said, or vow I vowed, or oath I swore – Your will is before all of that; what You willed was, and what You did not will was not; so forgive me and overlook it for me; O Allah, whom You blessed, my prayer is upon him, and whom You cursed, my curse is upon him – except he remains in his exception for the rest of that day."
"15649","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever swears and says: God willing – he has not broken."
"15650","Ibn Jurayj reported to us, who said: Ibn Tawus reported to me, from his father, who said: Whoever makes an exception has not broken, and he has the respite so long as he has not risen from his sitting."
"15651","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, who said: The exception in the oath is for as long as milking the abundant she-camel."
"15652","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata said to me: When he swears then excepts right after that, along with that, at that time – as if he says: So long as he has not cut off the oath and left it."
"15653","From al-Thawri, who said: If the speech is connected, he has his exception; and if he cuts it off and falls silent, then excepts – he has no exception; and the people hold this."
"15654","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mis'ar, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Allah, I shall surely raid Quraysh – then he fell silent, then said: God willing."
"15655","Ma'mar reported to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, who said: He has his respite so long as there is no speech between, when it is connected. Ma'mar reported to us, from al-Zuhri, as far as we know, the like of it."
"15656","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: When he excepts in himself, it is nothing, until he utters it with his tongue."
"15657","Ma'mar reported to us, from Hammad, who said: It is nothing until he makes himself hear."
"15658","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, about a man who says: His wife is divorced, God willing, if I do not do such-and-such – then does not do it – he said: His wife is not divorced, and there is no expiation upon him. Ma'mar said: And Hammad said that; from al-Thawri, from Ibn Tawus, and Hammad, the like of that."
"15659","From al-Thawri about a man who swears he will not do such-and-such unless he breaks – he said: When he breaks, the expiation falls upon him."
"15660","Ma'mar reported to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, who said: When he moves his tongue, it suffices him in the exception."
"15661","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr informed me, from his father, that he saw him discharging his oath concerning a beating he had vowed, with the lightest blow. Ata said: The Book of Allah has come down concerning that. He said: Take in your hand a bundle and strike with it and do not break your oath. A man said: In how many is that? He said: It has reached us that he had sworn to flog her a hundred lashes."
"15662","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, that a man committed an immoral act in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while he was sick on his death journey. He informed some of his family of what he had done, and he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took, or he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered, a palm raceme containing a hundred stalks, and he struck him with it a single blow."
"15663","From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Said, from Abu Umama ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, that a paralyzed man near the Harra of Sad committed adultery with a woman, and he confessed. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered that he be flogged with a palm-stalk bundle."
"15664","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: I said to him: A man swore to beat his slave. He said: To break the oath is dearer to me than that he beat him."
"15665","Ibn al-Taymi said: And I swore to beat a slave girl of mine who had fled, if I could get hold of her, and I found her. That reached my father, and he said: It has reached me that you swore that if you got hold of your slave girl you would beat her. It has reached me that the soul circulates in the body; sometimes its seat is the head, and sometimes its seat is in such-and-such place, until he listed places. So the blow may fall on it and destroy it, so do not do it. He said: So I did not beat her, and he did not order me to make expiation."
"15666","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Khallad, or another, informed me that a man swore falsely in the presence of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by Allah besides whom there is no god. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your false swearing has been forgiven for you on account of your sincerity, or something like that."
"15667","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told, from Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi, that a man stole a she-camel in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Its owner came and said: O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so stole my she-camel. I went to him and he refused to return it to me. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to him and said: Return to this man his she-camel. He said: By Him besides whom there is no god, I did not take it, and it is not with me. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go. When he turned his back, Jibril (peace be upon him) came to him and informed him that he had lied and that it was with him. So he sent to him: Let him return it. And he informed him: Allah the Exalted has forgiven him on account of sincerity."
"15668","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Ibn Umar saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of wala and its gifting."
"15669","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: Ali said: Wala is not sold and not gifted."
"15670","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: Ali said: Wala is like an alliance; it is not sold and not gifted. Leave it where Allah, Mighty and Majestic, placed it."
"15671","From Ibn Uyayna, from Mashar, from Abdullah ibn Maqil, from Ali, he said: Wala is a branch of lineage. Whoever secures wala secures inheritance."
"15672","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud was asked about the sale of wala, and he said: Would one of you sell his lineage?"
"15673","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying concerning the sale of wala, he said: I dislike that it be sold twice."
"15674","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Ata saying: Ibn Abbas used to dislike that wala be sold. He said: Would he consume by means of the neck of a free man? And he says: So neither the freed slave sells, nor the master who freed him. It is only like that. He said: I said to Ata: May his family sell his wala from himself? He said: No; that from him and from another is the same. He said: That repeatedly."
"15675","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Wala belongs to the one who freed; its sale and its gifting are not permitted."
"15676","From Ibn Jurayj, from Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Wala is not sold and not gifted."
"15677","From Mamar, he said: One who heard al-Hasan informed me, saying: Wala is lineage; it is not sold and not gifted."
"15678","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Wala is not sold and not gifted."
"15679","From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Wala is a joining like lineage; it is not sold and not gifted."
"15680","From Ibn Jurayj, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he used to dislike the sale of wala, and he disliked it with strong dislike, and that anyone should take as mawla other than his own mawalis, and that he should gift it."
"15681","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I gifted the wala of my mawla; is it permitted? He said: No, twice repeatedly. And I had heard him a while before saying: There is no harm in a man gifting the wala of his mawla. He said: I said: What distinguishes between his permitting him to take as mawla whom he wishes, for then he has gifted his wala to him, and gifting his wala to another? And every gifting. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever takes as mawla the freed slave of a people without their permission, upon him is the curse of Allah; no compensation nor recompense averts it from him."
"15682","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I permitted my mawla to take as mawla whom he wishes; is it allowed? He said: Yes. And Amr. Ata said: And it has reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that a man should take as mawla the freed slave of a people without their permission. And I had heard him a while before saying: When he permits his mawla to take as mawla whom he wishes, that is allowed."
"15683","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: Whoever takes as mawla a Muslim man without his permission, or shelters an innovator, upon him is the wrath of Allah; Allah accepts from him neither compensation nor recompense."
"15684","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote upon every clan its blood-money obligations."
"15685","Then he wrote: That it is not lawful for a Muslim to take as mawla the freed slave of a Muslim man without his permission. He said: I was informed that he cursed in his document whoever did that."
"15686","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Musa ibn Uqba informed me, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar used to disapprove that anyone should take as mawla other than his own mawla, and that he should gift it."
"15687","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever takes as mawla a Muslim freed slave without his permission, or shelters an innovator in Islam, or plunders a distinguished plunder, upon him is the curse of Allah; no compensation nor recompense averts it from him."
"15688","From al-Thawri, from Rabi ibn Abi Salih, from a man he named, he said: A man from the people of the land came to Ali wishing to take him as mawla, and he refused. So he came to Ibn Abbas and he took him as mawla. He said: His offspring today are many."
"15689","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: If he stipulates in his contract of manumission that I shall take as mawla whom I wish, it is allowed. And Qatada said: When the mukatab pays all that is upon him, let him take as mawla whom he wishes."
"15690","Mamar informed us, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man contracting a slave of his in manumission, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Stipulate his wala. He said: Qatada used to say: If he did not stipulate his wala, he takes as mawla whom he wishes when he is freed. Mamar said: The people rejected that from him."
"15691","From Ibn Uyayna and Mamar, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who takes a man as mawla, he said: His wala belongs to him, and he may transfer his wala where he wishes, so long as he has not paid blood-money on his behalf."
"15692","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha, she said: Barira came to Aisha seeking her help in her contract of manumission. Aisha said: What if I count out to them what they ask of you in one payment; will they sell you to me? So I free you. She said: Until I ask them. She went and asked them. They said: Yes, but the wala is ours. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came in to her and she mentioned that to him. He said: Buy her and free her, for wala belongs to the one who freed. So she bought her and freed her. She said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood as a preacher and said: What is with people who stipulate conditions not in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic? Whoever stipulates a condition not in the Book of Allah, his condition is void, even if he stipulates a hundred times. The condition of Allah is truer and surer."
"15693","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Abu al-Zubayr, that Urwa informed him, from Aisha, that she bought her as a mukataba for eight uqiyas, and nothing of her contract was reduced. Meaning Barira."
"15694","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Abi Mulayka saying: When Aisha bargained for Barira, she said: I shall free her. They said: And you shall stipulate for us her wala. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came in, and she said that to him. He said: Yes, stipulate it for them, for wala belongs to the one who freed. Then he stood as a preacher and said: What is with the condition before which the right of Allah has come? Wala belongs to the one who freed."
"15695","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwa narrated to me, from Aisha, she said: Barira came and said: I contracted with my people for nine uqiyas, one uqiya each year, so help me. Aisha said: If your people wish that I count it out to them in one payment, and your wala be mine, I shall do it. She went to her people and they refused. She came back from her people while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting, and said: I offered that to them and they refused unless the wala be theirs. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard that and asked her, and she informed him. He said: Take her and stipulate for them the wala, for wala belongs to the one who freed. So I did. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood as a preacher among the people, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: As for what follows: What is with people who stipulate conditions not in the Book of Allah the Exalted? It is void, even if a hundred conditions. The decree of Allah is truer, and the condition of Allah is surer."
"15696","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wala belongs to the one who freed."
"15697","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: Nafi narrated to us, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed that wala belongs to the one who freed."
"15698","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, that he was asked about a man and his son; one people freed the father, and other people freed the son. He said: They inherit one another by kinship, and wala remains upon those who freed."
"15699","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: The outcast, does he not take as mawla whom he wishes? He said: Yes. Saying: From Ibn Masud: He takes as mawla whom he wishes so long as he has not taken the former ones as mawla. He said: I said to Ata: The outcast attaches himself to a people but does not take them as mawla; they pay blood-money on his behalf and he pays on theirs, and they aid him, then he dies; whose is his inheritance? He said: Theirs. He said: I said: The outcast did not attach himself to anyone and did not take anyone as mawla, then he dies; likewise who inherits him? He said: The Muslims; their inheritance is in the treasury, and they pay blood-money on his behalf."
"15700","From al-Thawri, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir, from Masruq, he said: I came to Abdullah ibn Masud with a purse containing three hundred dirhams. He said: I said: Among us was a resident man who was killed in al-Daylam. Abdullah ibn Masud said: Has he any kin? I said: No. He said: Does anyone hold over him a contract of wala? I said: No. He said: Then show it here; there are many heirs here. Meaning the treasury."
"15701","From Qays ibn al-Rabi, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ziyad ibn al-Jarrah, that a man died and left seven hundred dirhams. Abdullah ibn Masud said: Has he anyone to take it? He said: Put it in the treasury of the Muslims, for he is one of the Muslims. From al-Thawri, from al-Rabi ibn Abi Salih, from a man he named, he said: A man came to Ali from the people of the land, like his first report."
"15702","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, decreed concerning a man who took a people as mawla: He made his inheritance theirs, and his blood-money upon them. Al-Zuhri said: If he did not take anyone as mawla, the Muslims inherit him and pay blood-money on his behalf."
"15703","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who says at his death: My mawla is so-and-so: His word is not acted upon unless he brings just proof contrary to what he said."
"15704","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man of the Arabs is among a people; no origin is known for him; they have paid blood-money on his behalf and he paid with them; then he dies; whose is his inheritance? He said: It has reached us that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Whoever used to become angry for him and protect him, his inheritance is his. And Amr ibn Dinar said it."
"15705","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Who pays blood-money on their behalf? He said: Those who inherit them. And I say: The position of the outcast is the same as this."
"15706","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and others, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote: That when he is in the register of a people who paid blood-money on his behalf, his inheritance is theirs."
"15707","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, he said: Amr ibn al-As wrote to Umar that a man whose register was among a people, and blood-money was paid on his behalf among them, died with no known heir. Umar wrote to him: If blood-money is paid on his behalf among them and his register is among them, pay his inheritance to them."
"15708","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed me, that with him on the day he informed me of this report was a letter from Umar ibn al-Khattab to Amr ibn al-As, that Amr had written to him asking him: What do you think concerning a man residing among a people, having no mawla of the Arabs, freed by no one; they pay blood-money on his behalf and aid him, and his hand is with their hands; he dies with no heir? He wrote to him: His inheritance is theirs. If he dies without taking anyone as mawla, without attaching himself, and leaving no heir, his inheritance is for the Muslims."
"15709","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed me, that a man of Banu Fihr in the pre-Islamic time was an evil man; his people disowned him. As for Islam, there is no disowning in it. So Amr ibn al-As took him as mawla, and between him and Amr was kinship through the women. The disowned man died and left a son of his, then that son died leaving no heir. Umar ibn al-Khattab decreed that his inheritance belongs to Amr ibn al-As."
"15710","From Mamar, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, that Ibn Masud said to a man: You, O company of the people of Yemen, when there dies among you a man whose Arab origin is not known, and it is not known from whom he is; whoever is like that and dies, he may bequeath all his wealth where he wishes."
"15711","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab decreed concerning a man who took a people as mawla: He made his inheritance theirs, and his blood-money upon them."
"15712","Malik informed us, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abu Jamila narrated to me that he found an abandoned infant in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab and brought him to him. Umar suspected him, then he spoke well of him, so Umar said: He is free, his wala is yours, and his maintenance is from the treasury."
"15713","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, that a man came to his family when they had picked up an abandoned infant. He went to Umar and mentioned it to him. Umar said to him: Perhaps the little cave holds calamity. The man said: They picked him up only while I was absent. Umar asked about him and he spoke well of him. Umar said: His wala is yours, and his maintenance is upon us from the treasury."
"15714","From al-Hasan ibn Umara, from al-Hakam, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, that Ali was asked about a foundling. He said: He is free; his blood-money is upon them, and his wala is theirs."
"15715","From al-Thawri, he said: The inheritance of the foundling, according to their companions, is in the treasury."
"15716","From al-Thawri, from Zuhayr ibn Abi Thabit, from Dhuhl ibn Aws, from Tamim, that he found a foundling and brought him to Ali, and he assigned him for a hundred."
"15717","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim and al-Shabi, they said: Concerning the foundling: He is free."
"15718","From Abu Hanifa, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, they said: If a man picks up a child of adultery and wishes to spend on him and have it as a debt upon him, let him call witnesses; and if he wishes to count it as reward with Allah, let him not call witnesses. Abu Hanifa said: I myself say: It is nothing unless the ruler assigns it for him upon him."
"15719","Ibn Jurayj and al-Hasan ibn Umara informed us, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, that a woman picked up a boy, then came to Shurayh seeking his maintenance. He said: You have no maintenance. He said: And his wala is yours."
"15720","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed us that Ibn Shihab informed him that he picked up a child of adultery and brought him to Umar ibn al-Khattab. He said: Go and have him suckled with the wealth of Allah, and his wala is yours. Ibn Shihab said: The man who picked him up and brought him to Umar ibn al-Khattab informed me of that himself."
"15721","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Awsaja, the mawla of Ibn Abbas, informed him, from Ibn Abbas, that a man died leaving no one to inherit him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek. They found no one to inherit him. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave his inheritance to a mawla of his whom the deceased had freed; he is the one to whom the wala belongs, he is the one who freed."
"15722","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Awsaja the mawla of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas, he said: A man died in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving no heir except a slave of his whom he had freed. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him his inheritance."
"15723","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ikrima ibn Khalid narrating that Umar ibn al-Khattab judged with the like of this judgment concerning a person for whom he found no heir except his freed mawla to whom the wala belonged: He gave the inheritance of the one who had freed to him."
"15724","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from Ikrima ibn Khalid, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab passed by the door of Nafi ibn Abd al-Harith, who was his governor over Mecca, and said: What happened to the blacksmith who was in this tent? They said: He died, O Commander of the Faithful. He said: Who inherits him? They said: You. He said: Why? There is between me and him neither kinship nor wala. Did he leave no one? They said: No, except that he bought a boy and freed him. He said: Give him his inheritance."
"15725","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, that a blacksmith in the quarter of Banu Jumah died leaving no heir except a slave he had freed. Umar ibn al-Khattab came to Mecca and that was referred to him, and he ordered that his inheritance be given to that slave who had been freed."
"15726","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: Umar and Abdullah used to give inheritance to the kin through women to the exclusion of the mawalis. He said: Ibrahim narrated to me, from Alqama, that a freed woman of his died leaving her maternal sister's son and leaving Alqama. So Alqama gave the wealth to her maternal sister's son. He said: And a freed woman of Ibrahim died, and the daughter of her paternal brother came, so he gave her the whole inheritance. She said: May Allah bless you. He said: Had it been mine I would not have given it to you."
"15727","From al-Thawri, he said: Mansur informed me, from Husayn, from Ibrahim, he said: Umar and Ibn Masud used to give inheritance to the kin through women to the exclusion of the mawalis. He said: I said: And Ali ibn Abi Talib? He said: He was the strictest of them in that."
"15728","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq informed me that Ziyad ibn Jariya informed Abd al-Malik that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to the commanders of Syria to learn archery, and to walk between the two targets barefoot, and to teach your boys writing and swimming."
"15729","While they were shooting, a boy passed and one of them struck him and killed him. He wrote concerning that to Umar, and he wrote: Find out whether there was between them any old blood-feud from the pre-Islamic time. The governor of Hims wrote: I wrote and found they were not exchanging. And he wrote to Umar that he had no known heir, nor kin except a maternal uncle. Umar wrote: His blood-money is for his maternal uncle; the maternal uncle is but a father. And he left the mawalis who had freed him."
"15730","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, he said: I heard in Medina that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah and His Messenger are the mawlas of the one who has no protector, and the maternal uncle is the heir of the one who has no heir. From Mamar, from Yala, from Mansur or Husayn, from Ibrahim, he mentioned the like of the report of al-Amash, from Ali, Umar and Abdullah, that he said that also. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from a truthful man, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
